var tafsirData_22 = {
  "1": "\"O humanity! Fear your Lord, for the ˹violent˺ quaking at the Hour is surely a dreadful thing\" opens the chapter of the pilgrimage, and it opens, unexpectedly, not with the pilgrimage but with the Hour, and the pairing is deliberate. The chapter is named for the great gathering at the Sacred House, and it begins with the greater gathering that every gathering points to. The call is to all humanity, the widest address in the Quran, and the command is \"Fear your Lord,\" the word 'taqwa' for the guarded awareness of God that shapes a person's whole life. The reason for the fear is stated in the second half: \"the ˹violent˺ quaking at the Hour is surely a dreadful thing.\" The word \"quaking\" is 'zalzalah,' the shaking that turns the earth loose from its foundations, and the verse describes it as a \"dreadful thing\", the word 'azim' for something enormous and overwhelming. The chapter's first stroke is the end of the world, because every journey in this world, including the journey to the pilgrimage, is a journey toward that hour.\n\nThe verse is the Quran's way of beginning a chapter with the scale of things. Before the pilgrimages and the sacrifices and the laws that follow, the reader is placed before the quaking of the Hour, so that everything said afterward is said in its shadow. The same shaking is described elsewhere: \"When the earth is shaken ˹in˺ its ultimate quaking,\" and \"˹Consider˺ the Day ˹when˺ the quaking Blast will come to pass.\" The chapter will return to the Hour again and again, and the first verse plants it as the background of everything. The fear commanded here is not terror alone; it is the fear that protects, the awareness that keeps a person from forgetting where they are going. The verse is a single sentence, and it contains the whole frame of the chapter: the world is moving toward a shaking, and the wise person lives in the awareness of it.",
  "2": "\"The Day you see it, every nursing mother will abandon what she is nursing, and every pregnant woman will deliver her burden ˹prematurely˺\" describes the Hour through the people who will see it, and the description is built from the strongest bonds the human heart knows. The nursing mother forgets the child at her breast, and the verse does not say she would forget for any ordinary fear; the sight of the Day itself is what breaks the bond. The pregnant woman delivers her burden before its time, and the verse says the delivery happens from the terror of the sight. Then the third picture: \"And you will see people ˹as if they were˺ drunk, though they will not be drunk\". The crowd will sway and stagger, and the verse adds the correction that makes the picture complete: they are not drunk, because no wine has touched them; what staggers them is the severity of what they are seeing. The verse then states the cause in four words: \"but the torment of Allah is ˹terribly˺ severe.\" The severity is the explanation, and the explanation covers all three pictures, the mother, the woman, and the staggering crowd.\n\nThe verse is the Quran's most human description of the Hour, and its power is in the details it chooses. It does not describe the sky or the mountains first; it describes the mother and her child, because if that bond breaks, every other bond has broken, and the reader can measure the Hour by the breaking. The same scene appears elsewhere in fragments: \"Then, when the Deafening Blast comes to pass\", and on that Day a person will flee from even their own brother. The verse teaches the reader to imagine the Hour not as a distant event but as a moment in which the strongest attachments of this world are torn, and the imagination is the first step of the fear that the previous verse commanded. The chapter has placed its reader inside the Day in two verses, and everything that follows will be read from inside that picture.",
  "3": "\"˹Still˺ there are some who dispute about Allah without knowledge, and follow every rebellious devil\" begins the chapter's portrait of the disputers, and the word \"Still\" connects them to the Hour of the previous verses: even with the quaking described, there are people who argue about God. The word \"dispute\" is 'jadal,' the argument of contention, and the verse says their arguing is \"without knowledge,\" the word 'ilm' for real knowledge, and the phrase is the diagnosis of their whole position: they argue about the One they have never truly sought to know. The second half of the verse names their guide: \"and follow every rebellious devil.\" The word \"rebellious\" is 'marid,' the devil who has thrown off every restraint, and the word \"every\" is striking: they do not follow one path of error but every path that presents itself, because a person without knowledge follows whatever pulls at him, and the pullers are many. The verse is the Quran's explanation of how arguing about God and following devils go together: the one who will not learn from revelation will be taught by the whisperers.\n\nThe verse also prepares the chapter's long argument about the resurrection, because the disputers it describes are the people who argued against life after death. The chapter will answer them with the stages of human creation and the revival of the earth, and this verse introduces them before the argument begins. The same portrait appears elsewhere: \"But there are some who employ theatrics, only to lead others away from Allah’s Way—without any knowledge—and to make a mockery of it.\" The verse is a warning about a certain kind of speech about God: the loud argument that carries no knowledge, and the confident opinion that follows the loudest voice. The next verse will show what has been decreed for such devils and their followers, and the decree explains why the disputer's freedom is an illusion: he is being led, and the leading has a destination.",
  "4": "\"It has been decreed for such devils that whoever takes them as a guide will be misguided and led by them into the torment of the Blaze\" states the law that governs the devils and their followers, and the verse is built on the word \"decreed\". The Arabic is 'kutiba alayhi,' it has been written against him, and the verse says the decree was fixed for every such devil: the one who takes him as a guide will be misguided by him and led into the Fire. The verse describes a chain with three links. The devil guides, and his guidance is misguidance, and the misguidance ends in the Blaze, and the chain cannot be broken by the follower, because the guide's whole purpose is the third link. The word \"guide\" is used with terrible irony: the devil is taken as a guide, and the guidance he gives is the one thing it cannot be, and the verse lets the irony stand. The follower chooses the devil because the devil promises a path, and the path is real, and it leads exactly where the verse says.\n\nThe verse is the Quran's teaching about the freedom and the bond of the follower of evil. The follower is not forced; he takes the devil as a guide by his own choice, and the verse says the choice is real and its consequence is written. The same pattern runs through the chapter: the disputers of the previous verse chose to argue without knowledge, and the verse now tells them what they have actually chosen. The commentators note that the verse speaks of \"such devils\" in general, so that the law covers every devil of every kind, the whisperers among the jinn and the whisperers among human beings, and the follower of any of them inherits the same destination. The verse is a warning in the form of a decree, and the next verse turns from the followers to the argument itself, the stages of creation that answer the doubt about the resurrection.",
  "5": "\"O humanity! If you are in doubt about the Resurrection, then ˹know that˺ We did create you from dust, then from a sperm-drop, then ˹developed you into˺ a clinging clot ˹of blood˺, then a lump of flesh—fully formed or unformed—in order to demonstrate ˹Our power˺ to you\" begins the chapter's great argument for the resurrection, and it is the argument from the reader's own beginning. The address is again to all humanity, and the verse meets the doubt directly: if the resurrection seems impossible to you, look at what has already happened. The stages are listed in order: dust, then the sperm-drop, then the clinging clot, then the lump of flesh, and the verse says the lump may be fully formed or unformed, and the purpose of the whole sequence is stated: \"in order to demonstrate ˹Our power˺ to you\". The stages of the embryo are the demonstration, and the demonstration is placed before the reader's eyes, because every human being is the walking proof of the power that made them. The same stages appear elsewhere: \"then We developed the drop into a clinging clot ˹of blood˺, then developed the clot into a lump ˹of flesh˺, then developed the lump into bones, then clothed the bones with flesh, then We brought it into being as a new creation.\"\n\nThe verse then completes the journey of the single life: \"˹Then˺ We settle whatever ˹embryo˺ We will in the womb for an appointed term, then bring you forth as infants, so that you may reach your prime.\" The embryo is settled in the womb for a term, and the term is appointed, and the infant is brought forth, and the child grows to full strength, and the verse traces the whole arc from dust to prime. Then the arc bends downward: \"Some of you ˹may˺ die ˹young˺, while others are left to reach the most feeble stage of life so that they may know nothing after having known much.\" The verse describes the full circle of human life, the rise and the decline, and the decline is stated with the same precision as the rise, because the One who knows the beginning knows the end. Then the last argument: \"And you see the earth lifeless, but as soon as We send down rain upon it, it begins to stir ˹to life˺ and swell, producing every type of pleasant plant.\" The earth that lies dead and then revives with rain is the resurrection displayed every season, and the verse places the dead earth beside the dead bones and asks the doubter to read the two together. The verse is one of the longest in the Quran, and every clause of it is one step in a single argument: the One who made you in stages and revives the earth every year can raise you after death.",
  "6": "\"That is because Allah ˹alone˺ is the Truth, He ˹alone˺ gives life to the dead, and He ˹alone˺ is Most Capable of everything\" draws the conclusion from the previous verse's stages, and the conclusion has three parts, each beginning with the word \"alone\". The first: \"Allah ˹alone˺ is the Truth\", and the word \"Truth\" is 'al-Haqq,' the real, the One whose existence is the ground of all existence, and the verse says the whole demonstration of the embryo and the rain points to Him, because only the true God could run the stages of life with such order. The second: \"He ˹alone˺ gives life to the dead\", and the verse states the power that the whole argument was about, the giving of life after death, and it states it as a fact, not a possibility, because the stages of the previous verse were the evidence. The third: \"He ˹alone˺ is Most Capable of everything\", and the verse closes with the attribute that covers all the rest, the power over every thing, so that the reader leaves the argument with the widest truth it supports. The same pattern appears elsewhere: \"He brings forth the living from the dead and the dead from the living. And He gives life to the earth after its death. And so will you be brought forth ˹from the grave˺.\"\n\nThe verse is the hinge of the chapter's argument, and its three clauses are arranged like the steps of a proof: the premise, the conclusion, and the ground. The commentators note that the verse answers the doubter in the language of certainty, because the stages of creation were not a theory but a memory: every listener had once been dust, then a drop, then a clot, and the memory of the beginning is the guarantee of the end. The verse also teaches the reader how the Quran argues: it does not ask for belief in the resurrection as a bare claim; it walks the reader through the evidence and then states the conclusion, and the conclusion carries the weight of everything that came before. The next verse will state the Hour itself, and the statement will need no further proof, because the proof has just been given.",
  "7": "\"And certainly the Hour is coming, there is no doubt about it. And Allah will surely resurrect those in the graves\" follows the argument with the announcement, and the announcement is framed in the strongest words the Quran uses for certainty. The word \"certainly\" is the word of assurance, and the verse says the Hour is coming, and the phrase \"there is no doubt about it\" closes every possible objection in the same breath, and the second half of the verse states the consequence: \"And Allah will surely resurrect those in the graves.\" The graves are named, because the grave is the doubter's strongest image, the place where the dead are sealed, and the verse says the graves will be opened and their people raised. The same announcement appears elsewhere: \"The Hour is certainly coming, there is no doubt about it. But most people do not believe.\" The verse does not argue here, because the argument was the previous verses; it announces, and the announcement is the fruit of the proof.\n\nThe verse also marks the transition of the chapter. The Hour has been announced, and the chapter will now return to the disputers and their fate, and the announcement stands behind everything that follows like a fixed point. The commentators note the two certainties in the verse: the coming of the Hour, and the resurrection from the graves, and the two are one event seen from two sides, the end of the world and the beginning of the judgement. The verse is the Quran's answer to the doubt it quoted at the start of the argument, and the answer is complete: the doubt was met with the stages of creation, and the stages led to the certainty, and the certainty is stated without a shadow. The reader who has followed the argument from the dust to the grave has been led, step by step, to the sentence this verse declares, and the sentence is the foundation of the rest of the chapter.",
  "8": "\"˹Still˺ there are some who dispute about Allah without knowledge, guidance, or an enlightening scripture\" returns to the disputers, and the verse repeats the charge of the earlier verse with two additions, and the additions are the point. The earlier verse said the disputers argue \"without knowledge,\" and this verse adds the two things that would have replaced the knowledge: \"guidance, or an enlightening scripture\". The word \"guidance\" is 'huda,' the direction that comes from following what God has shown, and the \"enlightening scripture\" is a book of clear light, the kind of revealed text that settles a matter, and the verse says the disputers have none of the three. They have no knowledge of their own, no guidance they have followed, and no scripture they can point to, and the verse lists the three absences so that the disputer's position is seen for what it is: an argument resting on nothing. The same list appears in the Quran's descriptions of the arrogant arguer, and the verse is the chapter's way of saying that dispute about God must be measured by its sources.\n\nThe verse also teaches the reader a standard for all speech about God, and the standard is the verse's three words: knowledge, guidance, and an enlightening scripture. The one who speaks about God should speak from one of these, and the one who speaks from none is the disputer the verse describes. The commentators note that the verse is aimed at the leaders of the disbelievers who argued against the Prophet in public, and their arguments were loud precisely because their sources were empty. The verse places the loudest voice of the disputer against the three quiet sources of truth, and the comparison does the work. The next verse describes the manner of such disputers, the pride and the turning away, and the manner will complete the portrait the sources have begun.",
  "9": "\"turning away ˹in pride˺ to lead ˹others˺ astray from Allah’s Way. They will suffer disgrace in this world, and on the Day of Judgment We will make them taste the torment of burning\" continues the description of the disputers, and the verse moves from their sources to their manner and their end. The first word is \"turning away,\" and the gesture is the proud turning of the neck, the refusal to even face the truth, and the verse says the turning is done \"in pride.\" The word for pride is 'itafan,' from the root of twisting the neck, and the commentators note the picture: the arrogant man literally twists himself away from what he does not want to hear. Then the purpose of the turning: \"to lead ˹others˺ astray from Allah’s Way\". The disputer is not content to be lost alone; his pride wants company, and the verse says he argues in order to pull others off the path with him, and the pulling is the worst part of his crime, because the one who misleads carries the sins of the misled as well as his own. The same portrait appears elsewhere, where the proud man is told to \"Fear Allah\", and then \"pride carries them off to sin\", and his resting place is the Fire.\n\nThe verse then states the two installments of the punishment: \"They will suffer disgrace in this world, and on the Day of Judgment We will make them taste the torment of burning.\" The word \"disgrace\" is 'khizyun,' the public shame, and the verse says it comes in this world, where the proud arguer's lies are eventually exposed and his followers eventually scatter, and the word \"taste\" for the torment of the Day is the word of direct experience, the punishment felt, not merely described. The verse is the complete case against the arrogant disputer: the empty sources, the proud manner, the misleading purpose, and the two-stage punishment, and the chapter places it between the argument for the resurrection and the verse that will tell the disputers what their own hands have earned. The next verse is spoken to them on the Day itself, and the voice that addresses them there closes the case.",
  "10": "˹They will be told,˺ \"This is ˹the reward˺ for what your hands have done. And Allah is never unjust to ˹His˺ creation\" is the sentence spoken to the disputers on the Day, and the first half of it is the simplest statement of justice in the Quran. The torment they are made to taste is described as the reward \"for what your hands have done\", and the word \"your hands\" ties the punishment to the deeds with the most direct image possible: the hands that argued, that wrote, that led others astray, are the hands that earned what they now receive. The verse does not say the punishment is arbitrary, and it does not say it is a misfortune; it says it is the reward of the deeds, and the equation is stated so plainly that no one can mistake it. The second half of the verse completes the justice: \"And Allah is never unjust to ˹His˺ creation.\" The word \"never\" is the Quran's strongest negation, and the verse says God's treatment of His creation is never injustice, so the punishment the disputers taste is the exact measure of what they sent ahead, neither more nor less. The same sentence appears almost identically elsewhere: \"This is ˹the reward˺ for what your hands have done. And Allah is never unjust to ˹His˺ creation.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's teaching about the fairness of the Hereafter, and it is placed in the middle of the warnings on purpose. Every threat in the chapter is framed as a reward of deeds, and every comfort is framed the same way, and the verse states the frame itself. The commentators note that the sentence is spoken \"to them\" on the Day, and the voice that speaks is not explained, because the speaker matters less than the truth spoken: the account is fair, and the fairness is the reason no complaint will be raised. The verse also answers the doubt that human hearts carry about God's justice, the fear that the scale might be tipped, and the answer is the verse's second half: He is never unjust to His creation, and the word \"never\" is the guarantee. The next verse turns to a different kind of person, the one who stands on the edge, and the chapter will show that the edge is the most dangerous place to worship from.",
  "11": "\"And there are some who worship Allah on the verge ˹of faith˺: if they are blessed with something good, they are content with it; but if they are afflicted with a trial, they relapse ˹into disbelief˺, losing this world and the Hereafter. That is ˹truly˺ the clearest loss\" introduces the edge-worshipper, and the portrait is drawn in two movements. The first describes his position: he worships God \"on the verge\", the word 'harf' for the edge of a cliff, and the image is exact: a person standing at the rim, one foot on the path and the other over the drop. The second movement describes how he behaves on the edge: \"if they are blessed with something good, they are content with it; but if they are afflicted with a trial, they relapse ˹into disbelief˺\". His worship is a business transaction, and the verse exposes the ledger: when the goods arrive, he stays, and when the trial arrives, he leaves, and the verse says the leaving is a relapse into disbelief, because a faith that is conditional on comfort was never settled in the heart. The same person appears elsewhere: \"There are some who say\" they believe in Allah, \"but when they suffer in the cause of Allah, they mistake ˹this˺ persecution at the hands of people for the punishment of Allah\".\n\nThe verse then states his account in one line: \"losing this world and the Hereafter. That is ˹truly˺ the clearest loss.\" The loss is doubled, and the doubling is the tragedy of his position: he did not gain the world by leaving, and he did not keep the Hereafter, and the verse says he lost both, and the loss is described as \"the clearest loss\", the word 'khusran mubin' for a loss so plain that no one can miss it. The commentators note the precision of the portrait: the edge-worshipper is worse off than the open disbeliever, because the disbeliever at least knows where he stands, while the man on the edge thought he was securing himself and secured nothing. The verse is the chapter's warning against the worship that is conditional, and the next verse will describe the gods such a person turns to when the trial comes, and the description will show how far the edge is from any real support.",
  "12": "\"They call besides Allah what can neither harm nor benefit them. That is ˹truly˺ the farthest one can stray\" describes where the edge-worshipper turns when the trial comes, and the verse states his new direction in one line. He calls on other than God, and the things he calls on are described by what they cannot do: they can neither harm nor benefit. The two negations cover everything a worshipper seeks from a god, the hope of gain and the fear of loss, and the verse says the called-upon have neither power, and the calling is therefore empty from its first word. The verse then measures the distance of this calling: \"That is ˹truly˺ the farthest one can stray.\" The word \"farthest\" is 'dalal ba'id,' a straying that is far away, and the verse says this is the extreme of being lost, not a small error but the outermost distance from the truth. The same sentence appears in the Quran's warning to its own Prophet: \"Do not invoke, instead of Allah, what can neither benefit nor harm you—for if you do, then you will certainly be one of the wrongdoers\", and the warning shows that the calling described here is the opposite of everything the chapter teaches.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's explanation of why the edge-worshipper's relapse is so complete. He did not fall from a high faith to a lesser faith; he fell from the edge to the farthest point, and the verse measures the fall by the distance between the two: the worship of the One who harms and benefits, and the calling on those who do neither. The commentators note the logic of the portrait: the man who worships for comfort will find, in the trial, that his gods offer no comfort, and the discovery is the farthest straying, because he has left the true support and found no other. The verse also corrects the assumption that such a person has simply changed his mind; the verse says he has strayed to the farthest distance, and the distance is the measure of the loss the previous verse announced. The next verse completes the picture with the verdict on the patrons he chose, and the verdict is the last word on the edge and its fall.",
  "13": "\"They invoke those whose worship leads to harm, not benefit. What an evil patron and what an evil associate!\" closes the portrait of the edge-worshipper with the verdict on his new gods, and the verse sharpens the previous one in a single phrase: \"whose worship leads to harm, not benefit.\" The previous verse said the false gods can neither harm nor benefit; this verse goes further, and the commentators explain the progression: the worship itself leads to harm, because the one who worships them earns punishment by the worship, so that the very act meant to secure him becomes the source of his ruin. The harm is not from the idols, which have no power; the harm is the consequence of the worship, the sin and the punishment it draws, and the verse says the worshipper's own act is his loss. The verse then closes with the exclamation that is the chapter's verdict on the whole business: \"What an evil patron and what an evil associate!\" The word \"patron\" is 'mawla,' the protector, and the word \"associate\" is 'ashir,' the close companion, and the verse condemns both roles of the false gods in the strongest exclamation the language has, the 'bi'sa' of contempt, the \"What an evil\" that leaves no room for doubt.\n\nThe verse is the end of the chapter's first section, and it gathers the three portraits the section has drawn: the disputer without knowledge, the arrogant leader of error, and the worshipper on the edge, and the exclamation at the end is aimed at all three, because all three took protectors and companions that failed them. The same parable appears elsewhere: \"The parable of those who take protectors other than Allah is that of a spider spinning a shelter. And the flimsiest of all shelters is certainly that of a spider, if only they knew.\" The verse is the chapter's way of closing the case before it opens the next section, the promise of the Gardens, and the closing is a warning: the edge has no railing, the patron has no power, and the only companion worth having is the One the chapter is about to describe. The next verse turns to the believers, and the turn is the chapter's own movement from the portraits of loss to the promise of the Gardens.",
  "14": "\"Indeed, Allah will admit those who believe and do good into Gardens, under which rivers\"flow opens the chapter's promise to the believers, and it comes immediately after the portraits of loss, so the contrast is the verse's first point. The chapter has just described the disputer, the proud leader, and the man on the edge, and now it turns to the ones who will enter the Gardens, and the two conditions of entry are named: belief and good deeds. The pairing is the Quran's constant formula, because faith without deeds has no fruit and deeds without faith have no root, and the verse keeps the two locked together. The Gardens are described with the phrase that runs through the Quran: \"under which rivers flow.\" The word for \"rivers\" is 'anhar,' and the picture is of water running beneath the gardens, the one thing the desert listener knew as the sign of life, and the verse makes it the floor of the promise. The same promise opens elsewhere: \"Give good news ˹O Prophet˺ to those who believe and do good that they will have Gardens under which rivers flow.\"\n\nThe verse then closes with the sentence that explains why the promise is certain: \"Surely Allah does what He wills.\" The sentence is the Quran's way of saying that the promise does not depend on the listeners' resources or the world's conditions; it depends on the will of God, and His will is not blocked by anything. The commentators note that the sentence also answers the doubters who measured the promise against their own power: the reward seems too great for human deeds, and the verse says the measure is not human, because God does what He wills, and His generosity is not limited by the scale of the deed. The verse is the first light in the chapter's long section of warnings, and it is placed first so that the reader hears the promise before the punishments that follow. The next verses will describe the two groups and their dispute, and this verse has already settled where the believers belong.",
  "15": "\"Whoever thinks that Allah will not help His Prophet in this world and the Hereafter, let them stretch out a rope to the ceiling and strangle themselves, then let them see if this plan will do away with ˹the cause of˺ their\"rage is one of the Quran's sharpest images, and the commentators explain its plain meaning: whoever is enraged at God's support of His Prophet, and thinks that support will not come, can do nothing about it except choke on his own rage. The verse speaks to the person who is angry at the truth, and the picture is of a man who hangs himself from his own ceiling in fury, and the verse asks him to see whether his self-destruction removes the cause of his rage. The cause of the rage, the support of God for His Prophet, remains untouched, and the one who tries to fight it only harms himself. The word \"strangle\" is the word of choking, and the commentators read the verse as a description of the inner state of the disbeliever: his anger against the message is a rope around his own neck, and the pulling tightens it, not the message.\n\nThe verse is also the chapter's answer to those who were enraged by the Prophet's victory, whether it came in this world or was promised for the Hereafter. The verse mentions both worlds: \"in this world and the Hereafter,\" and the mention closes the argument of those who said the Prophet would never prevail. The verse says his help is certain in both, and the one who cannot bear the certainty has only one way out, and the way out is his own destruction, and even that changes nothing. The commentators note the verse's tone: it is not a command to commit an act; it is a challenge that exposes the futility of rage against God's plan, the way one might tell a man who is fighting the tide to drown himself and see if the tide notices. The verse is the Quran's teaching that anger against the truth is a self-inflicted wound, and the wound heals nothing.",
  "16": "\"And so We revealed this ˹Quran˺ as clear verses. And Allah certainly guides whoever He\"wills continues the chapter from the rage of the previous verse to the source of the guidance itself, and the verse makes two statements about the Quran. The first is about its clarity: \"clear verses\", the word 'bayyinat' for signs that are plain and self-evident, and the verse says the Quran was revealed as such, so that no one can claim the truth was hidden or coded. The second statement follows: \"And Allah certainly guides whoever He wills.\" The two statements belong together, and the commentators explain the pairing carefully: the verses are clear, and the clarity is available to everyone, but the actual guidance, the opening of the heart that follows the clear signs, is God's gift, and He gives it to whom He wills. The same statement appears elsewhere: \"Indeed, We have sent down to you ˹O Prophet˺ clear revelations. ˹But˺ none will deny them except the rebellious.\"\n\nThe verse is the Quran's answer to a question that troubles every reader: if the truth is clear, why do some see it and some not? The answer is the verse's two halves together. The clarity is God's justice, and the guidance is God's grace, and the verse holds both in one sentence so that neither is denied. The one who receives guidance cannot boast, because the guidance was a gift, and the one who denies cannot complain, because the verses were clear, and the verse leaves the reader between the two truths with the right posture: gratitude for the gift, and no excuse for the rejection. The verse also prepares the chapter's next section, where God will judge between the believers and the other groups, and the judgement is described in the following verses. The clarity of the Book is the basis of the judgement, and the guidance of God is its secret.",
  "17": "\"Indeed, the believers, Jews, Sabians, Christians, Magi, and the polytheists—Allah will judge between them ˹all˺ on Judgment\"Day is the verse of the judgement between the religions, and it lists the groups of the Prophet's world by name. The believers are the Muslims, and then the verse names the Jews, the Sabians, the Christians, and the Magi, and then the polytheists, and the list covers the whole religious landscape of Arabia and beyond. The verse does not argue with any of the groups here; it states one fact that covers them all: \"Allah will judge between them ˹all˺ on Judgment Day.\" The word \"all\" gathers every group and every member, and the judgement is announced as a certainty, and the verse closes with the reason the judgement will be just: \"Surely Allah is a Witness over all things.\" The Judge is also the Witness, the One who saw everything each group did and said, and the verse places the disputes of the religions under the eye of the One who watched them from the beginning. The same principle appears elsewhere: \"Indeed, the believers, Jews, Christians, and Sabians—whoever ˹truly˺ believes in Allah and the Last Day and does good will have their reward with their Lord.\"\n\nThe verse is the Quran's teaching that the final word on the differences between religions does not belong to any of the disputing parties. Each group claims the truth, and the claims will be weighed, and the weighing belongs to God alone, and the verse tells the Prophet's community not to take the judgement into its own hands but to leave the other groups to the Judge who witnessed everything. The verse also warns the groups themselves: the differences they argue about are not forgotten, and the Day will sort them, and the sorting will be based on what God witnessed, not on what each group claimed. The verse is a call to humility for every side of the religious dispute, and the next verse will show the one fact that all the groups share whether they admit it or not: the whole creation bows to God, and the groups are part of that bowing.",
  "18": "\"Do you not see that to Allah bow down ˹in submission˺ all those in the heavens and all those on the earth, as well as the sun, the moon, the stars, the mountains, the trees, and ˹all˺ living beings\" opens the verse of the universal prostration, and the question \"do you not see\" is the Quran's way of saying that the evidence is in front of every eye. The verse describes a bowing that covers everything: all in the heavens, all on the earth, and then the list of the visible creation, the sun and the moon, the stars, the mountains, the trees, and every living being, and the verse says they all bow to God in submission. The commentators explain that the bowing of the creatures is their obedience to the order God placed in them, the way the sun keeps its course and the tree grows toward the light, and the verse calls that obedience a prostration, because every created thing follows the command it was given and cannot do otherwise. The same truth is stated elsewhere: \"The seven heavens, the earth, and all those in them glorify Him. There is not a single thing that does not glorify His praises—but you ˹simply˺ cannot comprehend their glorification.\"\n\nThe verse then adds the human exception, and it is the point of the whole description: \"as well as many humans, while many are deserving of punishment.\" Many human beings bow to God willingly, and many refuse, and the verse says the refusers deserve punishment, because the human being is the one creature whose bowing is a choice, and the choice makes the difference. Then the verse closes: \"And whoever Allah disgraces, none can honour. Surely Allah does what He wills.\" The one God disgraces for the refusal has no one to restore his honour, and the verse states the authority behind the whole scene. The verse is the Quran's picture of the true order of the universe: everything bows to God, the sun by its course and the believer by his will, and the only creature standing upright in refusal is the one whose uprightness is his own ruin. The same picture appears elsewhere: \"To Allah ˹alone˺ bow down ˹in submission˺ all those in the heavens and the earth—willingly or unwillingly—as do their shadows\", morning and evening.",
  "19": "\"These are two opposing groups that disagree about their\"Lord opens the chapter's description of the two parties, and the verse names them before it describes them: two groups, opposed, disputing about their Lord. The commentators explain that the two groups are the believers and the disbelievers, and the verse does not need to name them further, because the whole chapter has been drawing their two portraits. The dispute is about \"their Lord\", and the word is chosen carefully: He is the Lord of both groups, whether they accept Him or not, and the disagreement is about the One who owns them all. The verse then states what awaits the first group, the disbelievers: \"garments of Fire will be cut out for them and boiling water will be poured over their heads\". The word \"cut out\" is the word of tailoring, and the picture is of clothing being cut and fitted for them, and the clothing is fire, and the word \"garments\" keeps the image of wearing, so that the punishment is described as something they will be dressed in, the closest thing to the body, and then the boiling water is poured over their heads, and the two images, the garment of fire and the water of boiling, are the Quran's way of surrounding the body with the punishment from within and without. The same scene appears elsewhere: \"Then pour over their heads the torment of boiling water.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's announcement of the verdict on the dispute, and it is stated without argument, because the argument has already been made. The chapter has shown the signs of creation and the stages of human development, and the disputers have been described, and now the verse simply divides the two parties and their ends. The commentators note the structure of the next verses: the punishment of the disbelievers is described first, and the reward of the believers follows, and the order matches the chapter's movement from warning to promise. The verse is the first stroke of the description, the cutting of the garments, and the next verses will add the details of what the garments are made for and what the water does, and the reader is taken step by step into the scene.",
  "20": "\"melting whatever is in their bellies, along with their\"skin continues the description of the boiling water, and the verse states its effect on the body in two phrases. The first: \"melting whatever is in their bellies,\" and the word \"whatever\" makes the melting total, the organs and the insides, everything the belly holds, dissolved by the water that was poured over the head and reached the inside. The second: \"along with their skin\", and the skin is named because it is the body's boundary, the part that meets the world, and the verse says it melts with the rest, so that the punishment is described as reaching the inside and the outside together, the innermost organs and the outermost skin, and nothing of the body is left out of the description. The commentators note the verse's place in the sequence: the previous verse poured the water, and this verse states what the pouring does, and the two verses together form one picture of a punishment that surrounds and penetrates.\n\nThe verse is part of the Quran's method in describing the Fire, and the method is physical precision. The Quran does not say merely that the punishment is terrible; it names the belly and the skin, the melting and the pouring, and the naming is the warning, because the body that the listener inhabits is the body the verse describes. The same physical exactness appears elsewhere: \"The description of the Paradise promised to the righteous is that in it are rivers of fresh water... ˹Can they be˺ like those who will stay in the Fire forever, left to drink boiling water that will tear apart their insides?\" The verse also answers the doubt of those who said the Fire could not harm the body they know: the verse says it will melt the belly and the skin, and the melting is the proof that the body of the next world feels everything the verse describes. The next verse will add the third instrument of the punishment, the iron maces, and the description will be complete.",
  "21": "\"And awaiting them are maces of\"iron is the shortest verse of the passage, and its brevity is its power. After the garments of fire and the boiling water, the verse adds the third instrument: \"maces of iron\", and the word \"awaiting\" gives the scene its dread, because the maces are not thrown or wielded in a moment; they are waiting, prepared, and the waiting itself is part of the punishment. The word for \"maces\" is 'maqami',' the plural of the word for a heavy iron club, and the commentators explain that these are clubs of iron with which the people of the Fire are beaten, and the verse states their material, iron, because iron is the image of unyielding hardness, the metal that does not bend and does not break, and the verse leaves the rest to the imagination. The verse is one line, and the line is a complete sentence of punishment, and the Quran's method is visible in it: the shortest descriptions are often the most terrible, because they give the mind nothing to soften them with.\n\nThe verse is also part of the balance the Quran keeps in its descriptions of the Fire. The previous verses described the heat and the water, and this verse describes the blow, and the three together, the burning, the boiling, and the beating, are the Quran's way of saying that the punishment is not one pain but a surrounding of pains, each matching a sense. The commentators note that the verse's place between the boiling water and the next verse's escape attempts is deliberate: the punishment presses from every side, and the next verse will show that even the attempt to leave it is met and reversed. The verse is the middle of the chapter's portrait of the Fire, and the portrait will close with the words that the next verse records, the command to taste the burning, and the circle of the punishment will be complete.",
  "22": "\"Whenever they try to escape from Hell—out of anguish—they will be forced back into it, ˹and will be told,˺\"\"Taste the torment of burning!\" describes the last movement of the punishment, and it is the movement of the escape that fails. The verse says the people of the Fire will try to escape, and the reason for the attempt is named: \"out of anguish\", the word for the pain that drives a person to do anything to end it, and the attempt is real, and the verse then states its result: they are forced back into it. The word \"forced\" is the word of being returned against one's will, and the picture is of the escape being reversed, the way a hand pushes back what tries to leave, and the verse then records the voice that accompanies the return: \"Taste the torment of burning!\" The word \"taste\" is the Quran's word for direct experience, and the command is spoken to them, and the command is the punishment's own voice, telling them to experience what they denied. The same scene appears elsewhere: \"Whenever they try to escape from it, they will be forced back into it, and will be told, \"Taste the Fire's torment, which you used to deny.\"\"\n\nThe verse is the closing of the chapter's description of the Fire, and its lesson is the lesson of the closed door. The people of the Fire are not kept in it by walls alone; even their own attempts to leave are met and reversed, and the verse says the reversal comes from the anguish that drives the attempt, so that the escape is not a hope but another turn of the punishment. The commentators note the word \"anguish\" and its connection to the earlier verses: the disputers of the chapter lived in the anguish of their own pride, and now the anguish is the fire itself, and the attempt to escape it returns them to it, and the return is announced by the command to taste. The verse is the last stroke of the portrait of the two groups' first side, and the next verse turns to the second group, the believers, and the turn is the chapter's mercy, because the reader is not left inside the Fire but is led out of it to the Gardens.",
  "23": "\"˹But˺ Allah will surely admit those who believe and do good into Gardens, under which rivers flow, where they will be adorned with bracelets of gold and pearls, and their clothing will be silk\" turns the chapter from the Fire to the Gardens, and the word \"but\" is the hinge of the whole passage. The previous verses described the garments of fire and the boiling water and the iron maces, and now the verse describes the opposite in the same physical language: the believers will be adorned, and the word \"adorned\" is the word of jewelry, and the jewelry is \"bracelets of gold and pearls,\" and the clothing is silk, and every detail of the description answers a detail of the punishment. The garments of fire are answered by the clothing of silk, and the pouring of boiling water is answered by the rivers that flow beneath the Gardens, and the iron that awaited the disbelievers is answered by the gold and pearls that await the believers, and the verse is the Quran's way of showing that the Hereafter is one place with two halves, and the two halves are described in the same words so that the contrast is felt. The same description appears elsewhere: \"There they will be adorned with bracelets of gold, and wear green garments of fine silk and rich brocade, reclining there on ˹canopied˺ couches.\"\n\nThe verse also repeats the two conditions of the promise, belief and good deeds, and the repetition is deliberate: the same two words that opened the promise in verse fourteen close the contrast here, so that the reader knows exactly what separates the two groups. The commentators note that the verse's physical detail is a mercy to the imagination: the listener who cannot picture the unseen is given the most beautiful things he knows, gold and pearls and silk, and told that these are only the outer edges of what awaits. The verse is the first half of the believers' description, and the next verse will complete it with the reason they were given all of this, the guidance to the best of speech, and the reason will show that the adornment is the reward of something deeper than the adornment.",
  "24": "\"for they have been guided to the best of speech, and they have been guided to the Commendable Path\" explains why the believers were given what the previous verse described, and the reason is guidance. The verse names two things they were guided to, and both are about the direction of the life rather than its possessions. The first is \"the best of speech,\" and the commentators explain that this is the word of pure faith, the declaration that there is no god but Allah, the speech that is best because it is true and because it opens the door to everything else. The second is \"the Commendable Path\", and the commentators explain that this is the path of the praised Lord, the way of Islam, and the word \"Commendable\" connects the path to the One who is worthy of all praise, so that the believer's road is described by the one it leads to. The verse says they were guided to both, and the guidance is stated as a past fact, because their entry into the Gardens is the end of a road that began with the best of speech and ran along the commended path, and the verse is the Quran's way of saying that the adornment of the previous verse is the destination of the guidance, not a separate gift.\n\nThe verse also teaches the reader what to value while still on the road. The believers of this verse are not described as having been given gold and silk in the world; they were guided in the world, to the best of speech and the commended path, and the verse says the guidance is what carried them to the adornment. The same connection runs through the Quran's promises: the reward is described in the language of the senses, and its cause is described in the language of the heart, and the reader who wants the first is pointed to the second. The commentators note the verse's word order: the best of speech comes first, because the word of faith is the seed, and the path comes second, because the path is the growth, and the Gardens of the previous verse are the harvest. The verse is one line, and it contains the whole journey of the believer from the first word to the final home, and the next verse will turn to the people who blocked the road, and the contrast will measure what the blocking costs.",
  "25": "\"Indeed, those who persist in disbelief and hinder ˹others˺ from the Way of Allah and from the Sacred Mosque—which We have appointed for all people, residents and visitors alike—along with whoever intends to deviate by doing wrong in it, We will cause them to\"taste a painful punishment opens the chapter's section on the Sacred House, and it begins with a warning aimed at its blockers. The verse describes two crimes together: persisting in disbelief, and hindering others from the Way of Allah and from the Sacred Mosque, and the commentators explain the background: the pagans of Mecca had barred the Prophet and the believers from the Sacred Mosque, and the verse is God's warning to them that their blocking is recorded and punished. The word \"hinder\" is the word of turning people back and barring the road, and the verse joins the two crimes because they were one act in Mecca: the disbelief that drove the barring, and the barring that expressed the disbelief. The same crime is condemned elsewhere: \"Who does more wrong than those who prevent Allah’s Name from being mentioned in His places of worship and strive to destroy them?\"\n\nThe verse then states the principle that makes the crime so serious: the Sacred Mosque was appointed \"for all people, residents and visitors alike\". The word \"appointed\" means God made it a place of equal right, and the verse names the two groups, the resident and the visitor, so that no class of people can claim it as their own, and the one who bars any person from it is fighting God's appointment. The verse then adds the third crime: \"whoever intends to deviate by doing wrong in it,\" and the commentators explain that this covers every act of wrongdoing inside the sanctuary, because the sanctuary is a place where even the intention of evil is heavier than elsewhere. The verse closes with the punishment: \"We will cause them to taste a painful punishment.\" The verse is the chapter's first statement of the sanctity of the Sacred House, and the sanctity is stated as a law: the House belongs to all people by God's appointment, and the one who claims it for his own tribe, or bars the believer from it, or defiles it with wrongdoing, has set himself against the appointment, and the verse names his reward. The next verse will turn to the origin of the House, and the origin will show whose House it has always been.",
  "26": "“And ˹remember˺ when We assigned to Abraham the site of the House, ˹saying,˺ “Do not associate anything with Me ˹in worship˺ and purify My House for those who circle ˹the Ka’bah˺, stand ˹in prayer˺, and bow and prostrate themselves.”” records the assignment, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the assignment: “And remember when We assigned to Abraham the site of the House, saying, ‘Do not associate anything with Me in worship and purify My House for those who circle the Ka'bah, stand in prayer, and bow and prostrate themselves,’” or “wa ith bawwa’na li-Ibraheema makana al-bayti an la tushrik bee shay’an wa tahhir baytiya lit-ta’ifeena wal-qa’imeena war-rukka‘i as-sujood,” and the commentators note the assignment, for the Lord assigned the site of the House to Abraham, and the command is the purification, and the verse is the record of the assignment, the command that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the purification is the duty, and the worship is the purpose, and the verse is the record of the assignment, the command of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the site is the Kaaba, and the purification is the duty, and the verse is the record of the assignment, the command that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the purification, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own duty. The believer is taught that the purification of the House is the duty, and the one who purifies is the one whose duty the Lord values, and the believer who keeps the worship is the believer whose worship the Lord accepts, and the assignment to Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the purity is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the assignment is the believer whose duty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the duty of the purification: Abraham was assigned and purified, and the believer who keeps the worship is the believer whose duty the Lord values, and the assignment is the teaching that the purity is the thing that the House requires. The verse is the record of the assignment, and the teaching of the duty that the purification requires.",
  "27": "“Call ˹all˺ people to the pilgrimage. They will come to you on foot and on every lean camel from every distant path,” records the call, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the call: “Call all people to the pilgrimage. They will come to you on foot and on every lean camel from every distant path,” or “wa aththin fi an-nasi bil-hajji ya’tooka rijalan wa ‘ala kulli damirin ya’teena min kulli fajjin ‘ameeq,” and the commentators note the call, for the Lord commanded Abraham to call the pilgrimage, and the verse is the record of the call, the command that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the call is the command, and the coming is the answer, and the verse is the record of the call, the command of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the call is the command, and the coming is the answer, and the verse is the record of the call, the command that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the call, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own response. The believer is taught that the call of the Lord is the thing that the believer must answer, and the one who answers is the one whose response the Lord values, and the believer who performs the pilgrimage is the believer whose response the Lord accepts, and the call to Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the response is the thing that the believer must make, and the believer who reads the call is the believer whose response the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the answer of the call: Abraham called and the people came, and the believer who answers the call is the believer whose response the Lord values, and the call is the teaching that the response is the duty. The verse is the record of the call, and the teaching of the response that the pilgrimage requires.",
  "28": "“so they may obtain the benefits ˹in store˺ for them, and pronounce the Name of Allah on appointed days over the sacrificial animals He has provided for them. So eat from their meat and feed the desperately poor.”” records the purpose, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the purpose: “so they may obtain the benefits in store for them, and pronounce the Name of Allah on appointed days over the sacrificial animals He has provided for them. So eat from their meat and feed the desperately poor,” or “li-yashhadoo manafi‘a lahum wa yathkuroo isma Allahi fee ayyamin ma‘loomatin ‘ala ma razaqahum min baheemati al-an‘ami fa-kuloo minha wa at‘imoo al-ba’isa al-faqeer,” and the commentators note the purpose, for the pilgrimage is the benefits and the remembrance and the sacrifice, and the verse is the record of the purpose, the command that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the benefits are the rewards, and the sacrifice is the worship, and the verse is the record of the purpose, the command of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the benefits are the rewards, and the sacrifice is the worship, and the verse is the record of the purpose, the command that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the purpose, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own worship. The believer is taught that the worship is the remembrance and the sacrifice, and the one who remembers and sacrifices is the one whose worship the Lord values, and the believer who feeds the poor is the believer whose charity the Lord accepts, and the purpose of the pilgrimage is the teaching, the declaration that the worship is the thing that the believer must offer, and the believer who reads the purpose is the believer whose worship the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the worship of the pilgrimage: the remembrance and the sacrifice and the feeding, and the believer who performs the rites is the believer whose worship the Lord values, and the purpose is the teaching that the worship is the end of the pilgrimage. The verse is the record of the purpose, and the teaching of the worship that the pilgrimage requires.",
  "29": "“Then let them groom themselves, fulfil their vows, and circle the Ancient House.”” completes the rites, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the rites: “Then let them groom themselves, fulfil their vows, and circle the Ancient House,” or “thumma lyaqdoo tafathahum wal-yoofoo nuthoorahum wal-yattawwafoo bil-bayti al-‘ateeq,” and the commentators note the completion, for the pilgrim completes the rites, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rites that the following verse addresses, and the commentators note the significance, for the grooming is the completion, and the circling is the end, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rites of the pilgrimage. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the grooming is the completion, and the circling is the end, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rites that the following verse addresses.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own devotion. The believer is taught that the completion of the rites is the duty, and the one who completes is the one whose devotion the Lord values, and the believer who fulfills the vows is the believer whose devotion the Lord accepts, and the completion of the pilgrimage is the teaching, the declaration that the completion is the thing that the believer must do, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose devotion the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completion of the rites: the pilgrim completes and circles, and the believer who fulfills the vows is the believer whose devotion the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the devotion is the end of the pilgrimage. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the devotion that the rites require.",
  "30": "\"That is so. And whoever honours the rituals of Allah, it is best for them in the sight of their\"Lord opens the verse of the principle, and the phrase \"That is so\" closes the previous description and opens the law that follows. The word \"honours\" is 'yu'azzim,' to treat as great, and the verse says the one who treats God's rituals as great has done what is \"best for them in the sight of their Lord\", and the phrase \"in the sight of their Lord\" is the key, because the honouring is measured where no one sees it, in the sight of God, and the verse says the honouring is a gain for the one who does it, not a favour done to the rituals. The commentators explain that the rituals are everything God commanded in the pilgrimage, the places, the times, and the acts, and the honouring of them is the honouring of the One who commanded them, and the verse places the whole pilgrimage under this one principle: the pilgrim who treats the rites as great has the best of it with God. The same principle is stated elsewhere: \"And whoever honours the symbols of Allah, it is certainly out of the piety of the heart\", and the chapter will repeat it in a few verses.\n\nThe verse then turns to two laws that follow from the principle: \"The ˹meat of˺ cattle has been made lawful for you, except what has ˹already˺ been recited to you. So shun the impurity of idolatry, and shun words of falsehood.\" The cattle are lawful, and the exception is what the Quran has already forbidden, and the verse says the lawfulness is God's gift, not the pagans' invention, and then the two commands: shun the impurity of idolatry, and shun words of falsehood, and the commentators note the pairing, because idolatry is the impurity of belief and false speech is the impurity of the tongue, and the two are the sources of every other sin. The verse is the chapter's joining of the pilgrimage laws to the chapter's great theme: the pilgrimage is built on pure worship, and pure worship is built on truth, and the one who honours God's rituals honours them with a clean belief and a clean tongue. The same command appears elsewhere: \"All grazing livestock has been made lawful to you—except what is hereby announced to you and hunting while on pilgrimage.\" The next verse will state the principle of uprightness that underlies all of this, and the principle is the chapter's heart.",
  "31": "\"Be upright ˹in devotion˺ to Allah, associating none with Him ˹in\"worship˺ states the chapter's central command, and the word \"upright\" is 'hunafa',' the word that describes the original religion of Abraham, the pure devotion that inclines only to God. The word is the plural of 'hanif,' and the commentators explain that the hanif is the one who turns away from every false god and stands straight toward the one God, and the verse commands the believers to be exactly that: upright in devotion to God, associating none with Him in worship. The command is the heart of the chapter's pilgrimage section, because the pilgrimage itself is the act of the hunafa, the journey of the upright to the House of pure worship, and the verse places the inner state before the outer rites: the pilgrim who is not upright in devotion is circling a building, but the pilgrim who is upright is circling the House of his Lord. The verse then states the punishment of the opposite, and the statement is one of the Quran's most striking parables: \"For whoever associates ˹others˺ with Allah is like someone who has fallen from the sky and is either snatched away by birds or swept by the wind to a remote place.\" The picture has three parts: the fall from the sky, the body snatched by birds, and the remains swept by the wind to a distant place, and the commentators explain that the parable is the picture of the one who falls from the height of pure faith into idolatry: the fall is from the sky, and the end is the scattering, the body torn by birds and the rest carried by the wind, and nothing of the person is gathered or kept.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's most complete statement of its central theme, and the parable is its most unforgettable image. The one who associates others with God has fallen from the highest point a human being can stand on, and the fall ends in the scattering, and the verse leaves the picture in the reader's mind as the measure of what idolatry costs. The commentators note that the parable also describes the state of the idolater's life itself: his heart is divided among many gods, and the division scatters him the way the birds and the wind scatter the fallen, and the verse says the uprightness of the hanif is the only standing that holds. The verse is the hinge of the pilgrimage passage, and the next verses will return to the rites with this principle as their foundation, and the reader is meant to read the rites through the principle: the circling, the sacrifice, and the grooming are the movements of the upright, and the uprightness is what makes them worship.",
  "32": "\"That is so. And whoever honours the symbols of Allah, it is certainly out of the piety of the\"heart repeats the principle of the earlier verse with one addition, and the addition is the phrase that has made this verse famous: \"out of the piety of the heart.\" The word \"symbols\" is 'sha'a'ir,' the marks and rites of the religion, and the verse says the one who honours them does so out of the piety of the heart, and the commentators explain the meaning with care: the honouring of the rites is not judged by the size of the sacrifice or the beauty of the performance but by what is in the heart, and the word \"piety\" is 'taqwa,' the guarded awareness of God, and the verse says the awareness is what makes the honouring real. The verse answers the question that the previous verses might raise: what does God want from the pilgrim, the animal or the heart? The answer is the verse's one line: the honouring of the symbols comes from the piety of the heart, and the animal and the circling are the body of that piety, and God receives both only when the heart is in them. The same principle is stated in the Quran's other pilgrimage verses, and the commentators note that this verse is the summary of them all: the rites are the marks of the religion, and the marks are honoured by the heart, and the heart is where the religion lives.\n\nThe verse is also the chapter's teaching about the inner meaning of every outward act of worship, and the teaching applies far beyond the pilgrimage. The prayer, the fasting, the charity, and the pilgrimage are all symbols, and the verse says the honouring of them is from the piety of the heart, and the reader is taught to measure the acts by the heart that carries them, not the hands that perform them. The word \"certainly\" gives the verse the force of a fixed rule, and the rule is the chapter's answer to every kind of empty worship: the movement without the heart is not worship, and the heart without the movement is not enough, and the two joined are what the verse calls the honouring of the symbols. The verse closes the chapter's teaching on the inner side of the rites, and the next verse will close the outer side with the law of the sacrificial animals, and the two closings together complete the picture of the pilgrimage as body and heart.",
  "33": "\"You may benefit from sacrificial animals for an appointed term, then their place of sacrifice is at the Ancient House\" states the law of the sacrificial animals, and the verse has two halves that correspond to the two stages of the animal's life. The first half is the permission: \"You may benefit from sacrificial animals for an appointed term,\" and the commentators explain the benefit: the pilgrim may ride the animal, drink its milk, and make use of it until the appointed time of the sacrifice arrives, and the word \"appointed term\" is the known time of the rites, and the permission is a mercy that the pagan customs had forbidden, and the verse restores the gift that God had made lawful. The second half is the destination: \"then their place of sacrifice is at the Ancient House\", and the commentators explain that the place of sacrifice is the sacred area around the House, the place where the animals are finally offered, and the verse says the term of the animal's use ends there, at the House, and the ending is the meaning of the whole arrangement: the animal that served the pilgrim on the road is returned to God at the House, and the use and the sacrifice are one journey. The same law is stated elsewhere: \"Complete the pilgrimage and minor pilgrimage for Allah. But if prevented ˹from proceeding˺, then ˹offer˺ whatever sacrificial animals you can afford.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's closing of the laws of the sacrificial animals, and the closing teaches the reader the Quran's way with the animals of worship: they are provision and sacrifice together, the gift of God used with permission and returned with worship, and the verse keeps both halves of the arrangement in one sentence so that neither is lost. The commentators note the word \"Ancient House\" and its echo of the earlier verse: the animals are brought to the House that Abraham was commanded to purify, and the bringing is the pilgrim's participation in the ancient act, the sacrifice that has run through the House since its founding. The verse ends the chapter's pilgrimage section, and the next verse will turn to the chapter's second great theme, the description of the believers and their reward, and the turn will show that the pilgrimage and the reward are two parts of one religion, the journey to the House and the journey to the Gardens.",
  "34": "\"For every community We appointed a rite of sacrifice so that they may pronounce the Name of Allah over the sacrificial animals He has provided for them\" opens the verse of the shared rite, and it teaches that sacrifice is older than any single nation. The word \"rite\" is 'mansak,' the place and manner of worship, and the verse says God appointed a rite of sacrifice for every community, the earlier nations each having their own appointed way, so that the act of sacrifice itself runs through all of revelation, and the purpose is stated in one phrase: \"so that they may pronounce the Name of Allah over the sacrificial animals He has provided for them.\" The sacrifice exists so that God's name is spoken over the animals He gave, and the giving and the naming are the two halves of the rite: the animal is God's provision, and the name of God is the response, and the verse says the provision came first, because even the animal that is sacrificed is a gift before it is an offering. The verse then states the creed that unites all the rites: \"For your God is only One God, so submit yourselves to Him ˹alone˺.\" The rites may differ from nation to nation, but the God is one, and the verse draws the line the chapter has been drawing since its opening: the sacrifice is for the One God, and the submission is to Him alone.\n\nThe verse then closes with the promise to the people of the rite: \"And give good news ˹O Prophet˺ to the humble\". The word \"humble\" is 'mukhbitin,' the word for those who have lowered themselves before God and found rest in the lowering, and the verse announces good news to them, and the next verse will describe them in full. The commentators note the movement of the verse: the rite is appointed, the name of God is its purpose, the oneness of God is its creed, and the humble are its people, and the verse gathers the whole teaching of the sacrifice into four sentences. The verse is the chapter's way of saying that the pilgrimage and its sacrifices are not new inventions; they are the continuation of an appointment as old as the prophets, and the appointment has always pointed to the same God. The next verse will describe the humble to whom the good news is given, and the description is the chapter's portrait of the heart the sacrifice is meant to build.",
  "35": "\"those whose hearts tremble at the remembrance of Allah, who patiently endure whatever may befall them, and who establish prayer and donate from what We have provided for them\" describes the humble of the previous verse, and the description has four parts, each one a mark of the heart. The first is the trembling: \"whose hearts tremble at the remembrance of Allah,\" and the word for \"tremble\" is 'wajilat,' the fear that moves the heart, and the verse says the remembrance of God moves their hearts, and the commentators explain that this is the awe of love, the heart that is never settled in forgetfulness because the mention of God reaches it. The same description appears elsewhere: \"The ˹true˺ believers are only those whose hearts tremble at the remembrance of Allah, whose faith increases when His revelations are recited to them, and who put their trust in their Lord.\" The second mark is patience: \"who patiently endure whatever may befall them,\" and the word \"whatever\" covers every hardship, the loss and the pain and the trial, and the verse says they endure it all with patience, and the endurance is the fruit of the trembling heart, because the heart that fears God does not break under what God sends. The third mark is prayer: \"who establish prayer\", and the word \"establish\" means they hold the prayer up and keep it standing, not merely performing it but building their lives on it. The fourth mark is giving: \"and donate from what We have provided for them,\" and the verse says their giving comes from what God provided, and the word \"provided\" keeps the giving humble, because the giver knows he is passing on a gift.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's complete portrait of the people the sacrifice was meant to shape, and the four marks, the trembling, the patience, the prayer, and the giving, are the four directions of the believing heart: toward God, through the trial, in the worship, and to the needy. The same four appear in the opening of the second chapter: \"who believe in the unseen, establish prayer, and donate from what We have provided for them,\" and the repetition shows that the Quran's description of the believer does not change. The verse places this portrait in the middle of the pilgrimage laws, and the placement is the teaching: the circling and the sacrifice and the grooming are the movements, and the humble heart is what the movements carry, and the verse has just shown the reader what the carrier looks like. The next verse will return to the sacrificial animals themselves, and the description will complete the outer side of the rite.",
  "36": "\"We have made sacrificial camels ˹and cattle˺ among the symbols of Allah, in which there is ˹much˺ good for you\" states the status of the sacrificial animals, and the word \"symbols\" is 'sha'a'ir,' the same word the chapter used earlier for the rites, and the verse says the camels and cattle of the sacrifice are among God's symbols, the visible marks of the religion, and the honouring of them is part of the honouring of God. The verse then names the good in them: \"in which there is ˹much˺ good for you\", and the good is wide, the reward of the sacrifice, the meat that feeds, the milk and the riding before the sacrifice, and the verse keeps the word broad so that every benefit is included. Then the command of the rite: \"So pronounce the Name of Allah over them when they are lined up ˹for sacrifice˺.\" The word \"lined up\" is the picture of the animals standing in rows for the offering, and the command is to say the name of God over them, and the name of God is what turns the slaughter into worship. Then the verse states the two uses of the meat: \"Once they have fallen ˹lifeless˺ on their sides, you may eat from their meat, and feed the needy—those who do not beg, and those who do.\" The verse mentions both kinds of needy, the one who asks and the one who does not, so that neither is forgotten, and the command to eat is the correction of the pagan customs that forbade the meat, and the command to feed makes the sacrifice a table that reaches the poor. The verse then closes with the reason for the whole arrangement: \"In this way We have subjected these ˹animals˺ to you so that you may be grateful.\" The word \"subjected\" is the word of making submissive, and the verse says the animals were made submissive to human beings, and the purpose of the subjection is gratitude, and the sacrifice is the highest form of the gratitude, the gift returned to the Giver with His name spoken over it.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's fullest description of the sacrificial animals, and the description balances three things at once: the honour of the animals as God's symbols, the benefit of the animals for the people, and the gratitude that the subjection demands. The same balance runs through the Quran's teaching on every provision, and the verse places it in the middle of the pilgrimage, where the believer meets the animal and the balance is lived. The next verse will state the deepest truth about the sacrifice, the truth that reaches beneath the meat and the blood, and the statement will complete the teaching the verse has begun.",
  "37": "\"Neither their meat nor blood reaches Allah. Rather, it is your piety that reaches Him\" is the heart of the Quran's teaching on sacrifice, and it is one of the most quoted verses of the chapter, because it corrects a misunderstanding as old as worship itself. The verse says plainly that the meat and the blood of the sacrificed animal do not reach God, and the statement is aimed at every worshipper who imagined that the offering itself is what God receives, and the commentators note its historical target: the pagans used to smear the blood of their sacrifices on the walls of the Ka'bah, and the verse tells the believers that God is not reached by meat or blood but by what is in the chest. The second half of the verse states the positive: \"Rather, it is your piety that reaches Him\", and the word \"piety\" is 'taqwa,' the guarded awareness of God, and the verse says the awareness is what ascends to God, and the sacrifice is the occasion of the awareness, not its replacement. The same truth is told in the story of Adam’s two sons: \"Relate to them in truth ˹O Prophet˺ the story of Adam’s two sons—how each offered a sacrifice: Abel’s offering was accepted while Cain’s was not\", and the accepted offering was the one offered with devotion, and the verse of the chapter states the principle that the story illustrated.\n\nThe verse then explains the purpose of the subjection: \"This is how He has subjected them to you so that you may proclaim the greatness of Allah for what He has guided you to\". The word \"proclaim the greatness\" is 'takbir,' the saying that God is the greatest, and the verse says the animals were subjected so that the believer would proclaim God's greatness for the guidance he received, and the sacrifice becomes an act of announcing the truth rather than a transaction with the divine. The verse then closes: \"and give good news to the good-doers\", and the good-doers are those whose sacrifices carry the piety the verse described, and the good news is the chapter's promise to them. The verse is the Quran's permanent answer to every form of empty ritual: God is not fed by the offering, and the offering is not a payment, and the only thing that rises from the sacrifice is the heart that offered it, and the heart is what the verse has been describing since the chapter began.",
  "38": "\"Indeed, Allah defends those who believe. Surely Allah does not like whoever is deceitful, ungrateful\" opens the chapter's section on the believers' struggle, and the first sentence is a statement of standing protection: \"Allah defends those who believe.\" The word \"defends\" is 'yudafi'u,' the word of pushing back and repelling, and the verse says the defense of the believers is God's own action, and the commentators note that the verse is the introduction to the permission to fight that follows, and the introduction matters: the believers are not told to defend themselves first and hope for God's help; they are told that the defense is God's, and their struggle is the visible side of a defense that was always His. The second sentence states what the defense is against: \"Surely Allah does not like whoever is deceitful, ungrateful\", and the two words name the character of the enemy: the deceitful, the word 'khawwan' for the one who betrays every trust, and the ungrateful, the word 'kafur' for the one who denies the good done to him, and the verse says God does not love such people, and the words are aimed at the disbelievers of Mecca who broke their treaties and repaid kindness with hostility. The same promise appears elsewhere: \"We certainly help Our messengers and the believers, ˹both˺ in this worldly life and on the Day the witnesses will stand forth ˹for testimony˺\".\n\nThe verse is the foundation of everything the next verses will say about fighting, and the foundation is the character of the two sides: the believers are defended by God, and their enemies are deceitful and ungrateful, and the verse places the struggle inside the moral frame before any weapon is mentioned. The commentators note that the verse also teaches the believers what they must not become: the defense of God is for the believers, and the believer is not deceitful and not ungrateful, and the struggle that follows is the defense of the right, not the revenge of the wronged. The next verse will grant the permission to fight, and the permission will be worded as the verse has prepared: the wronged are allowed to fight back, and the allowance is the first of its kind in the Quran, and the chapter will explain why it came.",
  "39": "\"Permission ˹to fight back˺ is ˹hereby˺ granted to those being fought, for they have been wronged\" is one of the most important verses in the history of the Muslim community, because it is the first permission to fight, and the commentators record the moment it answered. For years in Mecca, the believers had been beaten, expelled, and killed while they were commanded to endure, and when they reached Medina and the persecution followed them, this verse came down and granted them the right to defend themselves, and the verse is worded with the care the moment deserved. The word \"permission\" is 'udhina,' the word of allowance, and the verse does not command fighting; it permits it, and the permission is given to \"those being fought,\" the ones who are already under attack, and the reason is stated in four words: \"for they have been wronged.\" The word \"wronged\" is 'zulimu,' the word of injustice, and the verse grounds the permission in the injustice they suffered, not in ambition or revenge, and the ground is the verse's whole teaching: fighting is permitted to the wronged, and only to the wronged, and the permission is a defense, not a beginning. The verse then closes with the assurance: \"And Allah is truly Most Capable of helping them ˹prevail˺.\" The word \"truly\" is the word of emphasis, and the verse says God is able to help them prevail, and the assurance is the answer to the believers' fear, because they were few and their enemies were the whole region, and the verse reminds them that the outcome is not measured by numbers. The same principle is stated elsewhere: \"Fight in the cause of Allah ˹only˺ against those who wage war against you, but do not exceed the limits.\"\n\nThe verse is the Quran's foundation for every law of armed struggle that follows, and the foundation has three stones: the permission is for the attacked, the reason is the wrong done to them, and the help is God's. The next verse will describe who these wronged people are and what was done to them, and the description will show that the permission was not a licence but a restoration, the right of the expelled to resist their expulsion, and the restoration is the chapter's definition of justice in the face of force.",
  "40": "\"˹They are˺ those who have been expelled from their homes for no reason other than proclaiming:\"\"Our Lord is Allah\" describes the people for whom the permission was granted, and the description is their case before history. They were expelled from their homes, and the verse says the expulsion had no reason except their declaration of faith, the words “Our Lord is Allah”, and the verse quotes the declaration as the entire cause of their suffering, and the contrast is the verse's point: they were driven out not for theft or violence but for a sentence, and the sentence is the simplest statement of the truth, and the verse places the sentence and the expulsion side by side so that the injustice is seen in full. Then the verse states the law that the expulsion made necessary: \"Had Allah not repelled ˹the aggression of˺ some people by means of others, destruction would have surely claimed monasteries, churches, synagogues, and mosques in which Allah's Name is often mentioned.\" The verse is famous for its list, and the list is the argument: the places of worship, the monasteries and churches and synagogues and mosques, all of them, would have been destroyed if God had not set some people to repel others, and the verse says the defense of the believers is what protects the places of worship of all, and the commentators note the order of the list, the mosque named last because the verse is addressed to the Muslims and the mosque is their own, and the naming of the others first shows that the protection extends to every house where God's name is mentioned. The same law is stated elsewhere: \"Had Allah not repelled a group of people by ˹the might of˺ another, corruption would have dominated the earth, but Allah is Gracious to all.\"\n\nThe verse then closes with the promise: \"Allah will certainly help those who stand up for Him. Allah is truly All-Powerful, Almighty.\" The word \"stand up\" is 'yansuruhu,' the word of helping, and the verse says God helps those who help Him, and the two names that close the verse, the All-Powerful and the Almighty, are the guarantee of the help. The verse is the chapter's complete justification of the permission to fight, and the justification has three parts: the wrong of the expulsion, the law of repelling aggression, and the promise of help, and the three parts together make the fighting a defense of every place where God is worshipped. The next verse will describe what these wronged people will do if God establishes them in the land, and the description will show that the fighters are the builders of prayer, not the seekers of power.",
  "41": "\"˹They are˺ those who, if established in the land by Us, would perform prayer, pay alms-tax, encourage what is good, and forbid what is evil\" continues the description of the wronged believers, and it answers the question that every grant of power raises: what will they do with it? The verse says: if God establishes them in the land, they will do four things, and the four are the constitution of their rule. The first two are the worship: \"perform prayer, pay alms-tax,\" the prayer that connects them to God and the alms that connect them to the poor, and the verse names them first because a community that cannot hold these two cannot hold anything. The second two are the command and the prohibition: \"encourage what is good, and forbid what is evil\", the two duties that the Quran places at the center of the believing community's mission, and the verse says the established believers will do both, the calling to the good and the standing against the evil, and the two are the public face of the prayer and the alms. The same two duties are stated elsewhere: \"Let there be a group among you who call ˹others˺ to goodness, encourage what is good, and forbid what is evil—it is they who will be successful.\" The verse then closes with the sentence that keeps the power in its place: \"And with Allah rests the outcome of all affairs.\" The word \"outcome\" is 'aqibah,' the end of things, and the verse says the end of every affair belongs to God, and the sentence is the boundary around the whole promise: the believers who are established in the land rule by the four duties, and the outcome they seek is not theirs to claim, because the end belongs to God.\n\nThe verse is the Quran's picture of the state the believers are to build, and the picture has been the standard of every Muslim ruler since. Power, in this verse, is not a prize but a trust, and the trust has four conditions, and the verse places the promise of establishment beside the description of the duties, so that the two are one: if the believers would do these four things, God establishes them, and the verse says the doing is the condition and the establishment is the gift. The same promise appears elsewhere in full: \"Allah has promised those of you who believe and do good that He will certainly make them successors in the land, as He did with those before them; and will surely establish for them their faith which He has chosen for them; and will indeed change their fear into security.\" The verse is the chapter's answer to the tyrants it described earlier: the rule of the believers is the rule of prayer and alms and goodness, and the end of all rule belongs to God. The next verse will turn to the deniers of the message, and the chapter will show that the struggle of the believers is one chapter in a long story of denial and response.",
  "42": "“If they deny you ˹O Prophet˺, so did the people of Noah before them, as well as ˹the tribes of˺ ’Ȃd and Thamûd,” records the first part of the consolation, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the consolation: “If they deny you O Prophet, so did the people of Noah before them, as well as the tribes of 'Ad and Thamud,” or “wa in yukaththibooka fa-qad kaththabat qablahum qawmu Noohin wa ‘Adun wa Thamood,” and the commentators note the consolation, for the Lord reminded the Prophet of the earlier denials, and the verse is the record of the consolation, the reminder that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the denial of the Prophet is the repetition of the pattern, and the verse is the record of the consolation, the reminder of the history. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the consolation is the reminder, and the denial is the pattern, and the verse is the record of the consolation, the reminder that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the consolation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the denial of the truth is the pattern, and the one who is denied is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the denial is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the consolation of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the denial is the thing that the truth always faces, and the believer who reads the consolation is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the denial: the prophets were denied and the Lord seized the deniers, and the believer who bears the denial is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the consolation is the teaching that the denial is the thing that the truth always faces. The verse is the record of the consolation, and the teaching of the patience that the denial requires.",
  "43": "“the people of Abraham, the people of Lot,” continues the consolation, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse continues the consolation: “the people of Abraham, the people of Lot,” or “wa qawmu Ibraheema wa qawmu Loot,” and the commentators note the continuation, for the verse adds the two peoples, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the reminder that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the two peoples are the two deniers, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the reminder of the history. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the continuation is the reminder, and the two peoples are the deniers, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the reminder that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the continuation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the denial of the truth is the pattern, and the one who is denied is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the denial is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the continuation of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the denial is the thing that the truth always faces, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the constancy of the denial: the peoples denied and were seized, and the believer who bears the denial is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the continuation is the teaching that the denial is the pattern. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the patience that the denial requires.",
  "44": "“and the residents of Midian. And Moses was denied ˹too˺. But I delayed ˹the fate of˺ the disbelievers ˹until their appointed time˺ then seized them. And how severe was My response!”” completes the consolation, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the consolation: “and the residents of Midian. And Moses was denied too. But I delayed the fate of the disbelievers until their appointed time then seized them. And how severe was My response!” or “wa ashabu Madyana wa kuththiba Moosa fa-amlaytu lil-kafireena thumma akhathtuhum fa-kayfa kana nakeer,” and the commentators note the completion, for the verse names Midian and Moses, and the completion is the reminder, the declaration of the delay and the seizure, and the verse is the record of the completion, the reminder that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the delay is the respite, and the seizure is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the completion, the reminder of the law. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the delay is the respite, and the seizure is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the completion, the reminder that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the law, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the delay of the Lord is the respite, and the one who is delayed is the one whose judgment the Lord brings, and the believer who trusts the law is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the delay is the thing that the judgment follows, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the law of the delay: the Lord delayed and seized, and the believer who trusts the law is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the delay is the prelude of the judgment. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the certainty that the law produces.",
  "45": "“Many are the societies We have destroyed for persisting in wrongdoing, leaving them in total ruin. ˹Many are˺ also the abandoned wells and lofty palaces!”” records the ruins, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the ruins: “Many are the societies We have destroyed for persisting in wrongdoing, leaving them in total ruin. Many are also the abandoned wells and lofty palaces!” or “fa-ka’ayyin min qaryatin ahlaknaha wa hiya zalimatun fa-hiya khawiyatun ‘ala ‘urooshiha wa bi’rin mu‘attalatin wa qasrin masheed,” and the commentators note the ruins, for the Lord declared the destruction of the wrongdoers, and the verse is the record of the ruins, the reminder that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the ruins are the witness, and the verse is the record of the ruins, the reminder of the end. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the ruins are the witness, and the destruction is the end, and the verse is the record of the ruins, the reminder that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the ruins, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reflection. The believer is taught that the ruins of the wrongdoers are the witness, and the one who reflects upon the ruins is the one whose reflection the Lord values, and the believer who reads the history is the believer whose reflection the Lord accepts, and the ruins of the verse are the teaching, the declaration that the wrong is the thing that the Lord destroys, and the believer who reads the ruins is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reflection of the ruins: the wrongdoers were destroyed, and the believer who reflects upon the ruins is the believer whose reflection the Lord values, and the ruins are the teaching that the wrong is the path of the ruin. The verse is the record of the ruins, and the teaching of the reflection that the history invites.",
  "46": "“Have they not travelled throughout the land so their hearts may reason, and their ears may listen? Indeed, it is not the eyes that are blind, but it is the hearts in the chests that grow blind.”” records the question, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the question: “Have they not travelled throughout the land so their hearts may reason, and their ears may listen? Indeed, it is not the eyes that are blind, but it is the hearts in the chests that grow blind,” or “a-fa-lam yaseeroo fi al-ardi fa-takoona lahum quloobun ya‘qiloona biha aw athanun yasma‘oona biha fa-innaha la ta‘ma al-absaru wa lakin ta‘ma al-quloobu allatee fi as-sudoor,” and the commentators note the question, for the Lord asked whether they travelled, and the verse is the record of the question, the teaching that the blindness is the heart, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the appeal, and the declaration is the diagnosis, and the verse is the record of the question, the teaching of the heart. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the appeal, and the blindness is the heart, and the verse is the record of the question, the teaching that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the blindness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reflection. The believer is taught that the blindness of the heart is the disease, and the one who reflects is the one whose heart the Lord opens, and the believer who reads the history is the believer whose reflection the Lord values, and the question of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the diagnosis of the blindness: the eyes see and the heart is blind, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose heart the Lord opens, and the question is the teaching that the reflection is the remedy of the blindness. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the reflection that the history invites.",
  "47": "\"They challenge you ˹O Prophet˺ to hasten the torment. And Allah will never fail in His promise. But a day with your Lord is indeed like a thousand years by your counting\" records the demand of the deniers and the answer, and the demand was the old one: bring the punishment now, if the promise is true, and the verse answers it in two sentences. The first sentence is the assurance: \"And Allah will never fail in His promise.\" The word \"never\" is the strongest negation, and the verse says the promise of the punishment will not fail, and the haste of the deniers will not change it, and the certainty of the promise is the answer to their doubt. The second sentence explains the apparent delay: \"But a day with your Lord is indeed like a thousand years by your counting.\" The verse measures God's days against human days, and the commentators explain the meaning: what feels like a long delay to human counting is nothing in the counting of God, and the punishment that seems late is exactly on time, because the time is measured by the One who promised, not by the ones who waited. The verse also answers the mockery in the demand: the deniers asked for haste as if the delay proved the promise false, and the verse says the delay proves only that their clock is not the clock that matters. The same answer is given elsewhere: \"They challenge you ˹O Prophet˺ to hasten the punishment. Had it not been for a time already set, the punishment would have certainly come to them ˹at once˺.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's teaching about the patience of God, and the teaching has two sides that the verse holds together: the promise is certain, and the timing is God's, and the one who denies the first because of the second has confused the clock with the promise. The commentators note the phrase \"a day with your Lord\" and its echo elsewhere: \"the angels and the ˹holy˺ spirit will ascend to Him on a Day fifty thousand years in length\", and the two verses together show that the measure of time changes with the measuring, and the human impatience that measures everything by its own short scale is the impatience the verse corrects. The verse is the answer to every generation that has asked why the punishment waits, and the answer is the same in every generation: the promise will not fail, and the day of the Lord is not your day, and the waiting that feels long to you is the blink of the One who promised. The next verse will state the same truth from the side of the delayed peoples, and the statement will close the chapter's teaching on the delay and the seizure.",
  "48": "\"Many are the societies whose end We delayed while they did wrong, then seized them. And to Me is the final return\" states the law of the delay in its plainest form, and the verse is the summary of everything the chapter has said about the destroyed peoples. The first half of the verse names the two movements that the previous verses described: the delay and the seizure, and it states them as a law: \"Many are the societies whose end We delayed while they did wrong, then seized them.\" The word \"many\" gathers all the names of the list, Noah's people and 'Ad and Thamud and Midian and the rest, and the verse says the same pattern ran through them all: they did wrong, and their end was delayed, and the delay was not a cancellation but a term, and then the seizure came. The verse uses the word \"while they did wrong\" to explain the delay itself: the time they were given was spent in the wrongdoing, and the spending is the reason the seizure was just, and the verse says it in God's own voice, \"We delayed\" and then \"seized\" them, and the voice is the verse's warning to its own audience, because the same delay is running for them. The second half of the verse closes the matter: \"And to Me is the final return.\" The word \"final\" is the word of the last destination, and the verse says every society, delayed or seized, returns to God, and the return is the true end of the pattern, because the seizure of this world is only the first installment, and the final return is where the full account is settled. The same truth is stated elsewhere: \"Those who disbelieve should not think that living longer is good for them. They are only given more time to increase in sin, and they will suffer a humiliating punishment.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's answer to the question that the delay always raises, and the answer is the verse's two halves: the delay is a term, and the return is the end, and the one who reads the two halves together understands that no wrongdoing is forgotten and no delay is forever. The commentators note that the verse is addressed to the Prophet as much as to the deniers: the Prophet is told that the pattern of the delay is the pattern he is watching, and the watching is itself the answer to his waiting, and the return to God is the horizon of every story the chapter has told. The next verse will turn to the Prophet's own task in the middle of the delay, and the task is stated in the simplest words: he is sent with a clear warning, and the warning is the mercy inside the delay.",
  "49": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺\"\"O humanity! I am only sent to you with a clear warning\" records the Prophet's commission in his own instructed words, and the verse is a boundary and a comfort at once. The address is to all humanity, the widest address in the Quran, and the word \"only\" draws the boundary: the Prophet is sent with a warning, and nothing more than the warning is claimed for him, and the verse answers every accusation that the Prophet sought power or wealth or worship, and the answer is the word \"only\". The warning is described as \"clear\", and the word 'mubin' means plain and self-evident, and the verse says the warning needs no interpreter and no addition, because its clarity is its proof, and the warning is the Quran's word for the message itself, the news of the account that is coming and the path that leads to safety. The verse is the Prophet's own definition of his mission, and the definition has been quoted by Muslims ever since as the boundary of every preacher: the messenger warns clearly, and the rest belongs to the hearer. The same commission is stated elsewhere: \"I am only sent to you with a clear warning\", and the repetition in the Quran marks the sentence as the summary of the prophetic office, and the office is the delivery of the warning, and the delivery is the mercy.\n\nThe verse is also the chapter's answer to the demand of the previous verses: the deniers asked for the punishment to be hastened, and the Prophet is told to answer with his commission, the clear warning, and the answer redirects the whole conversation: the question is not when the punishment comes but whether the warning has been heard, and the verse places the warning before the punishment and the hearer before the account. The commentators note the tenderness of the verse's form: the word \"only\" is the humility of the messenger, and the word \"clear\" is the generosity of the message, and the two together are the posture of every prophet: no claim for himself, and no obscurity in what he carried. The next verse states what the warning divides into, the two groups that every hearer joins, and the statement is the verse's promise and warning in one.",
  "50": "\"So those who believe and do good will have forgiveness and an honourable provision\" states the first side of the warning, and the verse is the promise that the warning carries. The two conditions are the chapter's constant pair: belief and good deeds, and the verse names them without elaboration, because the whole chapter has been elaborating them, and the promise follows: \"forgiveness and an honourable provision.\" The word \"forgiveness\" is 'maghfirah,' the covering and erasing of sins, and the verse names it first, because the believer's first need is the erasing of the past, and the word \"provision\" is 'rizq,' the word of sustenance, and the verse describes the provision as \"honourable\", the word 'karim' for what is generous and noble, and the provision is the reward of the Hereafter, the Gardens that the chapter described earlier, and the verse calls it a provision because the believer will be sustained by it the way the body is sustained by food, and the provision is honourable because it comes from the Generous One. The verse is the chapter's shortest statement of the believer's end, and the brevity is the point: after the long descriptions of the two groups, the end of the first group is two words, forgiveness and provision, and the two words contain everything.\n\nThe verse also teaches the reader the order of the believer's goods: forgiveness comes before provision, and the erasing comes before the giving, and the verse places them so, because the one whose sins are forgiven has received the greater gift, and the provision that follows is the extra, and the verse wants the believer to long for the first before the second. The commentators note that the verse is the answer to the fear that the warning of the previous verse might leave: the warning is clear, and the promise is equally clear, and the two are the two sides of the one message, and the verse states the promise in the present tense of certainty, \"will have\", because the promise is already written. The next verse states the other side, the end of those who fought the revelations, and the contrast will complete the chapter's division of the hearers.",
  "51": "\"But those who strive to discredit Our revelations, they will be the residents of the Hellfire\" states the second side of the warning, and the verse describes the opposite group in the chapter's strongest words. The word \"strive\" is 'sa'aw,' the word of running and straining, and the verse says the disbelievers strain themselves against the revelations, and the commentators explain the meaning: their effort against the Quran was not a passive doubt but an active campaign, the arguments and the mockery and the attempts to block others from it, and the word \"strive\" records the effort as the crime, because the one who merely doubts is not the one the verse describes, and the one who works against the truth has made the working his identity. The word \"discredit\" is 'mu'ajizin,' the word of trying to outrun and frustrate, and the verse says their striving was an attempt to defeat the revelations, and the attempt failed, and the verse states their end: \"they will be the residents of the Hellfire.\" The word \"residents\" is 'ashab,' the companions, and the verse uses it with the Fire, and the pairing is the Quran's word for the permanent dwellers, and the permanence is the point: the ones who strained against the truth for a lifetime will dwell with the Fire for the term that follows. The same verse appears elsewhere: \"As for those who strive to discredit Our revelations, it is they who will suffer the ˹worst˺ torment of agonizing pain.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's completion of the division that the previous verse began, and the division is the two ends of the two groups: the believers have forgiveness and provision, and the strivers have the Fire, and the verse places the two ends side by side so that the reader chooses between them with both in view. The commentators note the justice of the pairing: the strivers are not punished for their doubt but for their striving, the effort they poured into the fight against the truth, and the effort is what the verse records against them, and the recording is the Quran's warning to every age that the fight against the truth is the most expensive fight a person can wage. The next verse will turn to the subtle matter of the whispers that accompany the recitation, and the verse will explain how God preserves His revelation against the interference, and the explanation will comfort the Prophet in the middle of the striving.",
  "52": "\"Whenever We sent a messenger or a prophet before you ˹O Prophet˺ and he recited ˹Our revelations˺, Satan would influence\" ˹people's understanding of˺ his recitation. But ˹eventually˺ Allah would eliminate Satan's influence. Then Allah would ˹firmly˺ establish His revelations. And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise is one of the most discussed verses of the Quran, and its teaching is the preservation of revelation. The verse describes a pattern that ran through all prophecy: when a messenger recited God's revelations, Satan would interfere, and the commentators explain the interference in the strongest readings: Satan would cast whispers and doubts into the hearing, so that the listeners would misunderstand the recitation or be confused by it, and the verse says this happened to the prophets before, and the Prophet is being told that the interference is not new and not a sign against him. Then the verse states God's answer: \"But ˹eventually˺ Allah would eliminate Satan’s influence.\" The word \"eliminate\" is 'yansakh,' the word of removing and wiping out, and the verse says God removes what Satan cast, and then the third movement: \"Then Allah would ˹firmly˺ establish His revelations.\" The word \"establish\" is 'yuhkim,' the word of making firm and decisive, and the verse says the revelations are made firm and clear after the interference is removed, and the two movements, the removal and the establishment, are the verse's teaching: the whisper is temporary, and the revelation is permanent, and the pattern of the prophets is the proof. The verse then closes with the two names: \"And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise\", and the commentators explain the closing: God knows the interference as it happens, and His wisdom ordains that it be removed, and the preservation of the revelation is the work of both names. The same preservation is promised elsewhere: \"It is certainly We Who have revealed the Reminder, and it is certainly We Who will preserve it.\"\n\nThe verse has been the subject of a famous story in the books of commentary, the story of the cranes, and the reliable position of the scholars is that the story's strongest forms are not sound and conflict with the Quran's own statements, and the sound reading of the verse is the one just described: Satan's interference touched the hearing and the understanding of the recitation, never the revelation itself, and God removed the interference and established His verses, and the verse is the Quran's assurance that the Book in the reader's hands is the established revelation, purified of every whisper. The verse is the chapter's comfort to the Prophet in the middle of the striving of the previous verse: the strivers campaign, and Satan whispers, and God establishes, and the verse places the three in their true order, the whisper temporary, the campaign failing, and the establishment standing. The next verse will explain the purpose of the interference itself, and the explanation will show that even the whisper serves the trial that separates the hearts.",
  "53": "\"All that so He may make\" Satan's influence a trial for those ˹hypocrites˺ whose hearts are sick and those ˹disbelievers˺ whose hearts are hardened. Surely the wrongdoers are totally engrossed in opposition explains the purpose of the interference described in the previous verse, and the purpose is the trial. The verse says the whispers that touch the recitation are made a test, and the test separates two groups that the verse names by the condition of their hearts: \"those ˹hypocrites˺ whose hearts are sick\" and \"those ˹disbelievers˺ whose hearts are hardened.\" The sick heart is the heart with doubt in it, the disease that the Quran describes elsewhere, and the hardened heart is the heart that has closed itself completely, and the verse says the whisper works on both: the sick heart seizes the whisper as an excuse, and the hardened heart seizes it as a proof, and both are exposed by what they seize. The word \"trial\" is 'fitnah,' and the verse says the interference is a trial in the exact sense the chapter has used all along: it does not create the sickness or the hardness; it reveals them, and the revelation is the test's purpose, and the verse states the purpose in the word \"so\", the word that ties the interference to the trial and the trial to the hearts. The verse then closes with the verdict: \"Surely the wrongdoers are totally engrossed in opposition.\" The word \"opposition\" is 'shiqaq,' the word of splitting and standing against, and the verse says the wrongdoers are deep inside their opposition, and the word \"totally\" measures the depth, and the verse closes the chapter's long description of the disputers with their final state: opposition as a dwelling, not a position. The same trial is described elsewhere: \"We have appointed only ˹stern˺ angels as wardens of the Fire... so that those ˹hypocrites˺ with sickness in their hearts and the disbelievers will argue.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's teaching that the truth separates as much as it gathers, and the separation is the trial: the same recitation that opens the healthy heart closes the sick one, and the whisper that confuses the hardened heart leaves the believer firm, and the verse says the separation is the design, not the accident. The commentators note that the verse also explains why the interference was ever allowed: a truth that no one could resist would not test anything, and the test of the hearts is the purpose of the whole arrangement, and the verse says it plainly. The verse is the last word of the chapter's long section on the disputers, and the next verses will turn to the believers' call and the final scenes, and the turn will carry the chapter toward its closing: the call to God, the prostration, and the command to strive.",
  "54": "\"˹This is˺ also so that those gifted with knowledge would know that this ˹revelation˺ is the truth from your Lord, so they have faith in it, and so their hearts would submit humbly to it\" opens the verse of the knowledge, and it completes the purpose of the trial that the previous verse described. The whispers and the interference were made a test for the sick and the hardened, and the verse now states the other side of the same arrangement: the people of knowledge recognize the revelation through it, and the recognition is described in three movements. The first is the knowledge: \"would know that this ˹revelation˺ is the truth from your Lord,\" and the verse says the gifted ones know the truth of the revelation, and the knowing is not a guess but a certainty, and the word \"gifted\" keeps the knowledge a gift, not an achievement. The second is the faith: \"so they have faith in it,\" and the verse says the knowledge becomes faith, and the two are joined in the Quran's way, the knowing leading to the believing. The third is the humility: \"so their hearts would submit humbly to it\", and the word for the humble submission is 'tukhbit,' the lowering of the heart, and the verse says the hearts of the knowledgeable end in the lowering, because the more a person knows the revelation, the more the heart bows to it, and the bowing is the fruit of the knowing. The same recognition is described elsewhere: \"Those gifted with knowledge ˹clearly˺ see that what has been revealed to you from your Lord ˹O Prophet˺ is the truth, and that it guides to the Path of the Almighty, the Praiseworthy.\"\n\nThe verse then closes with the guidance: \"And Allah surely guides the believers to the Straight Path.\" The word \"Straight\" is 'mustaqim,' the word of the direct road, and the verse says God guides the believers to it, and the guidance is the chapter's summary of everything the knowledge and the faith and the humility lead to. The verse is the chapter's portrait of the other side of the trial: the same interference that trips the sick heart steadies the knowledgeable one, and the same revelation that the hardened heart fights is the one the humble heart bows to, and the verse says the difference between the two hearts is the guidance of God, and the guidance is the gift that the verse's first word named. The next verse will turn to the disbelievers and their persistence, and the turn will complete the contrast the chapter has drawn between the two responses to the revelation.",
  "55": "\"Yet the disbelievers will persist in doubt about this ˹revelation˺ until the Hour takes them by surprise, or the torment of a terminating Day comes to them\" describes the other side of the trial, and the verse states the end of the disbelievers' doubt. The word \"persist\" is the word of continuing, and the verse says their doubt will continue, not because the revelation is unclear but because they have chosen the doubt, and the verse then states the two things that will end it: \"until the Hour takes them by surprise, or the torment of a terminating Day comes to them.\" The word \"surprise\" is the word of the sudden arrival, and the verse says the Hour will come upon them while they are still inside their doubt, and the second option, \"the torment of a terminating Day\", is the punishment that cuts everything short, and the verse names both ends of the doubt: either the Hour itself, or the terminator that precedes it, and both are sudden, and the doubt has no conclusion of its own, because doubt never concludes; it is only interrupted. The same suddenness is described elsewhere: \"In fact, the Hour will take them by surprise, leaving them stunned. So they will not be able to avert it, nor will it be delayed from them.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's warning to the doubters in its plainest form, and the warning is that their state has an expiry that they do not control. The doubt that feels like a safe distance from the truth is, in the verse's picture, a waiting room with two exits, and both exits open suddenly, and the verse wants the reader to feel the insecurity of the position: the doubter is not standing still; he is persisting toward the interruption, and the persistence is the only thing he owns. The commentators note the two alternatives in the verse, the Hour and the terminating Day, and the explanation: the Hour is the great end of the world, and the terminating Day is the end of the individual, the death that cuts the doubt short, and the verse says the doubter will meet one or the other, and the doubt will not survive either. The verse is the chapter's answer to the doubters it has described since its opening, and the next verse will state what the arrival of the Hour brings, the sole authority of God and the judgement of the two groups.",
  "56": "\"All authority on that Day is for Allah ˹alone˺. He will judge between them. So those who believe and do good will be in the Gardens of Bliss\" opens the verse of the sovereignty, and the first sentence is the chapter's statement of the Hour's meaning. The word \"authority\" is 'mulk,' the kingdom and the rule, and the verse says the authority of that Day belongs to God alone, and the word \"alone\" closes every claim: the kings who ruled, the tyrants who commanded, and the disputers who argued are all stripped of their authority on that Day, and the verse states the stripping as the first fact of the Day. The same fact is stated elsewhere: \"True authority on that Day will belong ˹only˺ to the Most Compassionate. And it will be a hard day for the disbelievers.\" The second sentence states what the authority does: \"He will judge between them\", and the word \"between them\" gathers the two groups the chapter has been describing, the believers and the disputers, and the judgement is the settling of the dispute the chapter opened with. The third sentence states the verdict for the first group: \"So those who believe and do good will be in the Gardens of Bliss.\" The word \"Bliss\" is 'na'im,' the word of delight, and the Gardens of Bliss are the reward of the two conditions the verse names, the belief and the good deeds, and the verse places the believers in the Gardens in the same breath as the judgement, because the judgement of the believers is their reward, and the reward is the Gardens.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's summary of the Hour's outcome, and the summary has three parts that answer the three parts of the chapter's story: the authority, the judgement, and the reward, and the verse states them in their order, because the authority makes the judgement possible and the judgement makes the reward certain. The commentators note the word \"alone\" and its force: on the Day when every claimant is stripped, the believers are not left without a ruler, because the ruler is their Lord, and the verse says the Gardens of Bliss are the home of those who accepted the authority while it was still a matter of faith rather than sight. The verse is the promise that follows the warning of the previous verse, and the next verse will state the verdict for the second group, and the two verdicts together will close the chapter's long account of the two parties.",
  "57": "\"But those who disbelieve and deny Our revelations, it is they who will suffer a humiliating punishment\" states the verdict for the second group, and the verse names their two crimes in order. The first is the disbelief, the state of the heart that refused the truth, and the second is the denial of the revelations, the active rejection of the verses that were recited to them, and the verse joins the two because the denial is the disbelief made visible, and the verse then states their end: \"it is they who will suffer a humiliating punishment.\" The word \"humiliating\" is 'muhin,' the word of disgrace, and the commentators explain the choice: the punishment of the disbelievers is not only pain but humiliation, and the humiliation is the exact opposite of the pride that made them deny, and the verse says the ones who denied in arrogance will suffer in disgrace, and the two are the two ends of one arc: the pride that rose in this world and the disgrace that meets it in the next. The same punishment is described elsewhere: \"As for those who strive to discredit Our revelations, it is they who will suffer the ˹worst˺ torment of agonizing pain\", and the chapter has used the same words before, and the repetition is the verse's way of fixing the verdict in the reader's ear.\n\nThe verse is the second half of the judgement that the previous verse began, and the two halves are balanced with the chapter's usual precision: the believers have the Gardens of Bliss, and the deniers have the humiliating punishment, and the verse places the two verdicts side by side so that the reader sees the full scale of the judgement. The commentators note the word \"humiliating\" and its root: the same root that describes the dignity of the believer describes the disgrace of the denier, and the verse says the disgrace is the punishment's own face, the first thing the denier feels before the pain. The verse closes the chapter's account of the two parties, and the next verse will turn to the emigrants, the believers who paid the price of their faith in this world, and the turn will show that the Gardens of the next world are the repayment of the emigration of this one.",
  "58": "\"As for those who emigrate in the cause of Allah and then are martyred or die, Allah will indeed grant them a good provision. Surely Allah is the Best Provider\" opens the verse of the emigrants, and the verse reaches back to the believers the chapter described earlier, the ones who were expelled from their homes for saying that their Lord is Allah. The word \"emigrate\" is 'hajaru,' the word of leaving the homeland, and the verse names the emigration \"in the cause of Allah,\" and the phrase is the verse's measure of the act: the leaving was for God, and the leaving is what gives the act its value. The verse then covers both ways the emigrant's road can end: \"then are martyred or die,\" the one killed in the struggle and the one who dies naturally, and the verse places them together so that no emigrant is left out of the promise, and the promise follows: \"Allah will indeed grant them a good provision.\" The word \"provision\" is 'rizq,' and the verse describes it as \"good,\" and the commentators explain that the provision is the reward of the Hereafter, the Gardens the chapter has just named, and the word \"indeed\" gives the promise the force of certainty, and the verse closes with the name that guarantees it: \"Surely Allah is the Best Provider.\" The word \"Provider\" is 'ar-Razzaqin,' and the verse says God is the best of providers, and the comparison is the verse's comfort to the emigrants: they left their homes and their livelihoods, and the provider they left them for is the best provider, and the provision they will receive is the best provision. The same promise is stated elsewhere: \"As for those who emigrated in ˹the cause of˺ Allah after being persecuted, We will surely bless them with a good home in this world. But the reward of the Hereafter is far better, if only they knew.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's tribute to the emigrants of every age, and the tribute is a law: the one who leaves a place for God is not a loser, and the verse says the leaving has a provision attached to it, and the provision is better than what was left. The commentators note that the verse was revealed in the time of the first emigration, and the verse gave the emigrants what they needed most: the certainty that their leaving was seen and their provision was assured, and the certainty has carried every generation of emigrants since. The next verse will describe the place they will be admitted into, and the description is the verse's promise made visible.",
  "59": "\"He will certainly admit them into a place they will be pleased with. For Allah is truly All-Knowing, Most Forbearing\" describes the destination of the emigrants, and the verse's description of the place is built on the one word that matters to the traveler: \"pleased\". The verse does not describe the place by its rivers or its gardens, though the chapter has described them; it describes the place by the response of the one who enters it: \"a place they will be pleased with\", and the word \"pleased\" is 'yardaunaha,' the satisfaction of the heart, and the verse says the emigrants will be pleased with what they are admitted into, and the satisfaction is the verse's whole description, because a place that satisfies the heart needs no other description, and the heart's satisfaction is the only measure the verse uses. The verse then closes with the two names that explain why the satisfaction is certain: \"For Allah is truly All-Knowing, Most Forbearing.\" The All-Knowing is the One who knows what each emigrant left and what each heart needs, and the Most Forbearing is the One who did not rush to punish the emigrants' persecutors but waited, and the verse says the admission is the work of both names: the knowledge that prepares the right place for each one, and the forbearance that delayed the account until the place was ready. The commentators note the tenderness of the verse's construction: the verse does not say the emigrants will be admitted into gardens; it says they will be admitted into what pleases them, and the wording leaves the pleasure to the One who knows the hearts, and the reader is asked to trust the knowledge rather than imagine the details.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's completion of the promise to the emigrants, and the completion is a teaching about the Quran's descriptions of Paradise: the descriptions of rivers and silk are the outer edges, and the inner content is the satisfaction of the heart, and the verse places the inner content above the outer, because the emigrants did not leave their homes for rivers and silk; they left them for God, and the place that pleases them is the place where the leaving is repaid. The verse also carries a quiet word to the persecutors in the name Most Forbearing: the delay of their punishment was forbearance, and the forbearance has a term, and the term is the Day the previous verses described. The next verse will turn to the law of retaliation, and the law will show the balance the chapter places on the emigrants' dealings with those who wronged them.",
  "60": "\"That is so. And whoever retaliates in equivalence to the injury they have received, and then are wronged ˹again˺, Allah will certainly help them\" states the law of the equal response, and the verse is the chapter's teaching on justice in the middle of the struggle. The word \"retaliates\" is 'aqaba,' the word of answering a deed with its like, and the verse permits the one who was injured to answer with the equivalent of the injury, and the word \"equivalence\" is 'mithl,' the like, and the verse fixes the limit of the answer at the like, because the answer that exceeds the injury is a new wrong, and the verse permits the equal and forbids the excess in the same breath. The verse then adds the case that calls for God's help: \"and then are wronged ˹again˺, Allah will certainly help them.\" The commentators explain the meaning: the one who answered his wrongdoer with the equal response, and is then wronged again for the answering, has God's help against the second wrong, and the verse says the help is certain, and the word \"certainly\" is the assurance. The same law is stated elsewhere: \"If you retaliate, then let it be equivalent to what you have suffered. But if you patiently endure, it is certainly best for those who are patient\", and the verse of the chapter states the permission while the other verse names the better option, and the two together are the Quran's complete teaching on the matter: the equal response is allowed, and the pardon is better. The verse then closes with the two names that frame the law: \"Surely Allah is Ever-Pardoning, All-Forgiving\", and the names are the verse's hint at the better path, the pardoning and the forgiving that the law permits and the names praise, and the commentators note the placement: the verse permits the retaliation and then closes with the names of pardon, so that the reader hears the permission and the invitation together.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's balance for the believers in the struggle, and the balance has two arms: the wronged may answer, and the answer must be equal, and the verse places the law between the promise to the emigrants and the signs of creation that follow, and the placement is the teaching: the justice of the struggle is part of the larger order of God, and the next verse will turn to that order, the merging of the night and the day, and the turn will show that the law of the equal response is one thread in the fabric of God's arrangement.",
  "61": "\"That is because Allah causes the night to merge into the day, and the day into the night. Indeed, Allah is All-Hearing, All-Seeing\" explains the help of the previous verse through the order of the world, and the verse points to the merging of the night and the day as the reason. The word \"merge\" is 'yuliju,' the word of entering and folding, and the verse says God folds the night into the day and the day into the night, and the commentators explain the meaning of the connection: the One who runs the great order of the world, the alternation of the light and the dark, is the One who helps the wronged, and the help is as certain as the alternation, and the verse says \"That is because\", tying the help to the power that runs the sky, so that the believer reads the sunrise as the guarantee of the help. The verse then closes with the two names that match the situation: \"Indeed, Allah is All-Hearing, All-Seeing\", and the pairing is exact: the wronged believer is heard in his complaint and seen in his injury, and the verse says the hearing and the seeing are the source of the help, and the one who is heard and seen by God is never abandoned. The same alternation is described elsewhere: \"You cause the night to pass into the day and the day into the night. You bring forth the living from the dead and the dead from the living.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's way of grounding the promise of help in the order of creation, and the grounding is the Quran's method: the believer is not asked to trust the help as an isolated promise; he is asked to look at the night and the day, the order that never fails, and to read the help as part of the same order, and the verse says the two are one because the One who runs the sky is the One who hears the complaint. The commentators note the beauty of the verse's logic: the merging of the night and the day is the most visible of God's actions, and the help of the wronged is the most hidden, and the verse joins them so that the hidden is believed through the visible, and the believer who has watched the dawn has seen the guarantee of the help. The next verse will state the truth that underlies both, the truth of God and the falsehood of the idols, and the statement will complete the chapter's chain of reasons.",
  "62": "\"That is because Allah ˹alone˺ is the Truth and what they invoke besides Him is falsehood, and Allah ˹alone˺ is truly the Most High, All-Great\" states the deepest reason of all, and the verse is the chapter's creed in its most compact form. The first sentence sets the two sides of existence: \"Allah ˹alone˺ is the Truth,\" and the word \"Truth\" is 'al-Haqq,' the real, the One whose existence is the ground of everything, and the second half of the sentence sets the opposite: \"what they invoke besides Him is falsehood,\" and the word \"falsehood\" is 'batil,' the unreal, and the verse says the things they call upon are unreal, and the two sentences are the whole argument of the chapter: the truth is one, and the rest is falsehood, and the help of the previous verse flows from the truth, because the truth is the side that God supports. The verse then closes with the two names that crown the creed: \"Allah ˹alone˺ is truly the Most High, All-Great\", and the word \"truly\" gives the names the force of certainty, and the Most High is the One above every claim, and the All-Great is the One beyond every measure, and the verse says the truth of God is the height and the greatness, and the falsehood of the idols is the lowness and the smallness, and the two names are the verse's final word on the matter. The same creed appears elsewhere in almost the same words: \"That is because Allah ˹alone˺ is the Truth and what they invoke besides Him is falsehood, and ˹because˺ Allah ˹alone˺ is the Most High, All-Great.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's gathering of its reasons, and the gathering is the Quran's teaching that the promises and the warnings all rest on this one fact: God is the truth, and the rest is falsehood, and the verse places the fact at the end of the chain so that the reader sees the chain's foundation. The commentators note the verse's repetition of the word \"alone\", and the repetition is the verse's way of closing every door to partnership, and the reader who has followed the chapter from the Hour to the pilgrimage to the struggle arrives at this verse and finds the whole journey summarized: the truth is one, and the help is for the side of the truth, and the height and the greatness belong to the One who is the truth. The next verse will turn to the signs of the truth, the rain and the green earth, and the signs will be the visible proof of the creed the verse has just stated.",
  "63": "\"Do you not see that Allah sends down rain from the sky, then the earth becomes green?\"opens the verse of the rain, and the question is the chapter's recurring appeal to the visible, the \"Do you not see\" that runs through the Quran's arguments. The verse points to the simplest miracle the reader knows: the rain comes down from the sky, and the earth becomes green, and the verse states the two facts without elaboration, because the elaboration is unnecessary, and the sequence, the rain and the green, is the chapter's proof of the power that the previous verses described, and the word \"green\" is the verse's single word of colour, the colour of life, and the verse places it at the end of the question so that the reader sees the colour before the argument. The verse then closes with the two names that match the scene: \"Surely Allah is Most Subtle, All-Aware.\" The word \"Subtle\" is 'Latif,' the One who acts with gentleness and precision, and the commentators explain the pairing with the rain: the rain that falls and the earth that greens are the work of the Subtle, because the water reaches the seed and the seed opens and the green appears through a gentleness that no force could replace, and the All-Aware is the One who knows every seed and every drop, and the verse says the green earth is the visible signature of the two names. The same scene is described elsewhere: \"Do you not see that Allah sends down rain from the sky—channelling it through streams in the earth—then produces with it crops of various colours\".\n\nThe verse is the chapter's turn from the creed to the signs, and the turn is the Quran's method: the truth is stated, and then the truth is shown, and the rain is the showing, and the reader who has seen a desert turn green has seen the argument of the verse with his own eyes. The commentators note the word \"then\" in the verse, the word that joins the rain to the green, and the joining is the argument: the green does not come from the earth alone, and the rain does not fall by itself, and the sequence is the work of the One the verse names, the Subtle and the Aware. The verse is the first of a series of signs that will run through the next verses, and the next verse will state the ownership behind the signs, the ownership of the heavens and the earth, and the statement will complete the argument the rain began.",
  "64": "\"To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. Allah ˹alone˺ is truly the Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy\" states the ownership that the previous verse's rain displayed, and the verse is the chapter's shortest argument against the idols. The first sentence is the claim: everything in the heavens and everything on the earth belongs to God, and the word \"whatever\" leaves no exception, and the claim is stated as a fact, not an argument, because the chapter has been arguing it since its opening, and the reader who has followed the rain and the green and the merging of the night and the day knows the fact already. The second sentence draws the conclusion from the ownership: \"Allah ˹alone˺ is truly the Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy.\" The word \"Self-Sufficient\" is 'al-Ghani,' the One who needs nothing, and the verse says God is the rich one, and the ownership is the proof of the richness, because the owner of everything needs nothing from anyone, and the word \"Praiseworthy\" is 'al-Hamid,' the One who is praised, and the verse says the ownership deserves the praise, and the two names together are the chapter's answer to the idols: the idols own nothing and need worshippers, and God owns everything and needs nothing, and the verse places the two sides of the matter in one sentence. The same statement appears elsewhere: \"O humanity! It is you who stand in need of Allah, but Allah ˹alone˺ is the Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's teaching on the difference between the Creator and the worshipped, and the difference is the verse's two names: God is self-sufficient, and the idols are not, and the verse says the praise belongs to the One who needs nothing, and the worship of the needy is the inversion that the chapter has been correcting throughout. The commentators note the verse's place in the sequence of signs: the rain showed the action, and the ownership states the owner, and the self-sufficiency draws the conclusion, and the three verses together are one argument, and the reader who has read them has been led from the green earth to the praiseworthy Lord. The next verse will add the third sign, the subjection of the earth and the sea and the sky, and the subjection will complete the chapter's description of the world as a gift arranged by its owner, and the arrangement is the proof of the ownership the verse has just stated.",
  "65": "\"Do you not see that Allah has subjected to you whatever is in the earth as well as the ships ˹that˺ sail through the sea by His command? He keeps the sky from falling down on the earth except by His permission\" opens the verse of the subjection, and the verse gathers three of the world's great services in one sentence. The first is the earth itself: \"Allah has subjected to you whatever is in the earth\", and the word \"subjected\" is 'sakhkhara,' the word of making submissive, and the verse says everything in the earth, the animals and the metals and the plants, was made submissive to human beings, and the subjection is stated as God's doing, not the human achievement, and the reader is asked to see the whole earth as a servant placed at his service. The second is the sea: \"as well as the ships ˹that˺ sail through the sea by His command\", and the verse names the ships because the ship is the human work that rides the sea, and the verse says the riding is by God's command, and the sea that could swallow the ship carries it instead, and the carrying is the subjection. The third is the sky: \"He keeps the sky from falling down on the earth except by His permission\", and the verse points to the most unnoticed of the services, the sky held up above the earth, and the verse says its holding is God's doing, and the phrase \"except by His permission\" keeps the power in God's hand, and the sky that shelters the reader every day is held by the One the verse describes. The verse then closes with the name that matches the gifts: \"Surely Allah is Ever Gracious and Most Merciful to humanity\", and the commentators note the pairing: the subjection is the grace, and the naming of humanity is the mercy, and the verse says the gifts are the work of the two names, and the reader is addressed as the receiver of the grace and the mercy. The same subjection is described elsewhere: \"Have you not seen that Allah has subjected for you whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth, and has lavished His favours upon you, both seen and unseen?\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's fullest description of the world as a gift, and the description is the argument: the earth, the sea, and the sky are three servants of one master, and the reader who lives on the earth and crosses the sea and shelters under the sky is surrounded by the evidence of the One who subjected them all, and the verse asks the reader to see the subjection as the signature it is. The next verse will turn to the reader's own life, the giving of life and the taking of it, and the turn will complete the circle of the signs, from the world to the self, and the completion will state the chapter's verdict on the human response to all of it: ingratitude.",
  "66": "\"And He is the One Who gave you life, then will cause you to die, and then will bring you back to life. ˹But˺ surely humankind is ever ungrateful\" turns the signs from the world to the self, and the verse states the three stages of the human being's own existence. The first: \"gave you life,\" and the verse says the life itself was given, and the giving is the first fact the reader never earned, and the verse begins the list with the gift that makes the list possible. The second: \"then will cause you to die,\" and the verse says the death that awaits every reader is God's doing, and the statement is the verse's answer to the one who imagined that death is the end of God's involvement, and the verse places the death between the two lives as the middle of the arrangement. The third: \"and then will bring you back to life\", and the verse states the resurrection as the third stage of the same arrangement, and the three stages, the giving, the taking, and the returning, are the chapter's complete account of the human journey, and the verse says the One who began the journey will complete it, and the completion is the return that the chapter has been announcing throughout. Then the verse states the human response to the arrangement: \"˹But˺ surely humankind is ever ungrateful.\" The word \"ungrateful\" is 'kafur,' the word of the denier, and the verse says the human being meets the three gifts with denial, and the word \"ever\" makes the denial a habit, and the verse's verdict on humanity is the contrast that the whole verse is built on: the gifts are three, and the response is one, and the one is denial. The same account is stated elsewhere: \"How can you deny Allah? You were lifeless and He gave you life, then He will cause you to die and again bring you to life, and then to Him you will ˹all˺ be returned.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's diagnosis of the human condition in one line, and the diagnosis is the verse's last word: the denial of the resurrection is not a failure of evidence but a failure of gratitude, and the one who denies the third stage has already denied the first, because the same hand that gave the life will return it, and the verse asks the reader to read the three stages as one act and one promise. The commentators note the verse's placement after the signs of the world: the earth and the sea and the sky were the outer signs, and the verse now turns to the inner sign, the reader's own life, and the inner sign is the closest and the most ignored, and the verse says the ignoring is the ingratitude it names. The next verse will turn to the different ways of the communities, and the turn will show that the ingratitude has a history, and the history is the chapter's answer to the disputes about the ways of worship.",
  "67": "\"For every community We appointed a code of life to follow. So do not let them dispute with you ˹O Prophet˺ in this matter. And invite ˹all˺ to your Lord, for you are truly on the Right Guidance\" states the law of the different ways, and the verse is the chapter's answer to the disputes between the religions. The first sentence is the law: \"For every community We appointed a code of life to follow\", and the word \"code\" is 'mansak,' the word the chapter used for the rites of sacrifice, and the verse says every community was given its own appointed way, the laws and the rites that fit its time, and the appointment is stated as God's doing, so that the differences between the communities are not accidents but assignments, and the verse places the differences inside the plan rather than against it. The same law is stated elsewhere: \"To each of you We have ordained a code of law and a way of life. If Allah had willed, He would have made you one community, but His Will is to test you with what He has given each of you.\" The second sentence is the instruction that follows the law: \"So do not let them dispute with you ˹O Prophet˺ in this matter,\" and the verse tells the Prophet not to be drawn into the arguments about the differences, because the differences are settled by the appointment, and the disputing about them is the old disputing the chapter has been condemning. The third sentence is the positive task: \"And invite ˹all˺ to your Lord, for you are truly on the Right Guidance\", and the verse turns from the dispute to the invitation, and the invitation is to the Lord, not to a code, and the verse says the Prophet's own position is the Right Guidance, and the word \"truly\" is the assurance, and the verse teaches the caller the order: leave the dispute, and make the invitation.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's map for the believer among the religions, and the map has three stops: the ways were appointed, the disputes about them are to be left, and the invitation to the Lord is the task, and the three stops together are the Quran's teaching on the plurality of the ways: the differences are real, and the dispute is useless, and the call is the response. The commentators note the word \"invite\" and its contrast with \"dispute\": the dispute is the fight about the ways, and the invitation is the call to the One who appointed them, and the verse says the believer's energy belongs to the second, not the first. The next verse will state what to say when the argument is pressed, and the statement is the shortest answer in the chapter, and the brevity is the point.",
  "68": "\"But if they argue with you, then say,\"\"Allah knows best what you do\" records the answer the Prophet is told to give when the argument continues, and the answer is the chapter's shortest, and its shortness is its teaching. The verse says: if they argue, say that God knows best what they do, and the sentence ends the argument by referring it upward, and the commentators explain the wisdom: the arguer who will not accept the invitation is not argued with but handed to the One who knows, and the handing is the believer's release from the endless dispute, and the verse says the release is a command, not a weakness, because the one who knows everything is the best judge, and the judge's knowledge is the end of the argument. The sentence \"Allah knows best what you do\" is one of the Quran's formulas of closure, and the verse places it here as the full answer, and the reader is taught that there is a point where the believer stops arguing and starts entrusting, and the point is reached when the argument has become a performance rather than a search. The same closure appears throughout the Quran, and the verse is the chapter's version of it, and the version is the verse's whole content.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's teaching on the end of the dispute, and the teaching has two sides that the verse holds in its two halves: the dispute is met with the truth as long as it is a search, and when it becomes a fight, the fighter is handed to God, and the verse says the handing is the believer's part, and the judgement is God's, and the two parts are the verse's complete answer to the arguers the chapter has been describing. The commentators note the word \"do\" in the sentence: the verse does not say God knows best what they say, because the argument is words, and the words are the smallest part of the matter, and the verse says God knows what they do, the deeds behind the arguments, and the knowledge of the deeds is the knowledge that matters. The next verse states the judgement that follows the entrusting, and the statement completes the chapter's answer to the disputes.",
  "69": "\"Allah will judge between you ˹all˺ on Judgment Day regarding your differences\" states the judgement that the previous verse entrusted, and the verse is the chapter's final word on the disputes between the religions. The word \"judge\" is 'yahkumu,' the word of ruling, and the verse says God will judge between all the parties, and the word \"all\" gathers every disputant of every religion, and the judgement is placed \"on Judgment Day\", the Day the chapter has described in its opening and its middle, and the verse says the differences will be settled there, and the settling is the reason the believer can leave the dispute now, because the dispute has a court date, and the court is the Day, and the judge is God, and the verse wants the reader to feel the relief of the arrangement: the argument does not have to be won today, because it has already been scheduled for the Day where it will be settled. The verse is the chapter's answer to the previous verse's entrusting: the believer says that God knows best, and the verse says the knowing will become a judging, and the judging will be on the Day, and the three together are the complete release from the endless dispute. The same judgement is described elsewhere: \"True authority on that Day will belong ˹only˺ to the Most Compassionate. And it will be a hard day for the disbelievers.\"\n\nThe verse is also a warning folded into a comfort, and the warning is for the disputers: the differences they argue about are not forgotten, and the Day will not be a continuation of the argument but its verdict, and the verdict will be based on the knowledge that the previous verse stated, and the verse says the arguing will end where it always had to end, before the Judge who saw everything. The commentators note the verse's placement after the argument-answer: the believer's silence in the dispute is not an admission but a postponement, and the postponement is to the Day, and the verse says the Day will settle what the world could not. The next verse will state the knowledge that makes the judgement possible, the record of the heavens and the earth, and the statement will close the chapter's argument about the judgement.",
  "70": "\"Do you not know that Allah ˹fully˺ knows whatever is in the heavens and the earth? Surely it is all ˹written˺ in a Record. That is certainly easy for Allah\" opens the verse of the Record, and the verse states the knowledge that makes the judgement of the previous verse possible. The first sentence is the question: \"Do you not know that Allah ˹fully˺ knows whatever is in the heavens and the earth?\" and the word \"fully\" closes every gap, and the question is asked as a reminder of what the reader already knows, and the verse says the knowledge covers everything in the heavens and the earth, and the coverage is the foundation of the judgement, because a judge who does not know everything cannot judge everything, and the verse states the coverage first. The second sentence names the form of the knowledge: \"Surely it is all ˹written˺ in a Record\", and the word \"Record\" is 'kitab,' the book, and the verse says everything is written in a book with God, and the writing is the verse's answer to the fear that the details might be lost, and the book is the Quran's image of the perfect preservation, and the commentators explain that the book is the record of every deed and every event, and nothing in the heavens or the earth falls outside it. The third sentence states the ease of it all: \"That is certainly easy for Allah\", and the verse says the knowledge of everything is easy for God, and the ease is the verse's answer to the one who measures God's knowledge by human effort, and the verse says the measurement is false, because the One who created the heavens and the earth does not strain to record them. The same record is described elsewhere: \"With Him are the keys of the unseen—no one knows them except Him... Not even a leaf falls without His knowledge, nor a grain in the darkness of the earth or anything—green or dry—but is ˹written˺ in a perfect Record.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's statement of the foundation of the judgement, and the foundation has three stones: the knowledge, the record, and the ease, and the three together make the Day of the previous verse possible, and the verse says the possibility is certain, because the foundation is already laid, and the book is already written. The commentators note the verse's tone: the question at the opening is a rebuke of the forgetful, and the ease at the closing is the rebuke's comfort, and the reader is asked to trust the record rather than fear the forgetting. The next verse will turn to the idols with the knowledge of the verse behind it, and the turn will ask the question the whole chapter has been asking: what authority do the idols have, and the question will close the chapter's argument against them.",
  "71": "\"Yet they worship besides Allah that for which He has sent down no authority, and of which they have no knowledge. The wrongdoers will have no helper\" states the verdict on the idols in the light of the previous verse's record, and the verse names the two absences that condemn the worship. The first absence is the authority: \"that for which He has sent down no authority,\" and the word \"authority\" is 'sultan,' the proof and the warrant, and the verse says God sent down no warrant for the idols, and the absence of the warrant is the verse's first argument, because a worship that God has not authorized is a worship that has no ground, and the verse says the authority would have to come from above, and none came. The second absence is the knowledge: \"and of which they have no knowledge\", and the verse says the worshippers have no knowledge of what they worship, and the two absences together are the complete case: the worship has no warrant from God and no knowledge from the worshipper, and the verse says the act rests on nothing, and the word \"yet\" at the opening sets the absurdity of the situation: with the record and the knowledge of God just described, they still worship what has neither warrant nor knowledge. The verse then closes with the consequence: \"The wrongdoers will have no helper\", and the word \"helper\" is 'nasir,' the one who stands beside, and the verse says the worshippers of the unauthorized will find no helper on the day when helpers are needed, and the absence of the helper is the mirror of the absence of the warrant, and the verse says the two absences meet at the end. The same question is asked elsewhere: \"Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, \"Have you considered whatever ˹idols˺ you invoke besides Allah? Show me what they have created on earth!\"\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's final argument against the idols, and the argument is the simplest the chapter has made: the worship has no warrant and no knowledge, and the worshipper will have no helper, and the three negations are the verse's whole case, and the case needs no further evidence because the previous verses supplied it, and the verse states the verdict on the foundation they built. The commentators note the word \"wrongdoers\" and its place: the verse does not call them disbelievers here but wrongdoers, because the worship of the unauthorized is an injustice, the placing of worship where it does not belong, and the injustice is the verse's final name for the act. The next verse will describe the rage of the disbelievers at the recitation, and the description will show the inner state of the wrongdoers, the rage that the verse's verdict provokes.",
  "72": "\"Whenever Our clear revelations are recited to them, you ˹O Prophet˺ recognize rage on the faces of the disbelievers, as if they are going to snap at those who recite Our revelations to them\" describes the response of the disbelievers to the recitation, and the verse is the Quran's portrait of rage as a reaction to truth. The word \"rage\" is 'munkar,' the word of the disapproving and the hostile, and the verse says the rage is recognizable on their faces, and the word \"recognize\" addresses the Prophet's own seeing, because he watched the faces change when the Quran was recited, and the verse says the recitation itself is what provokes the rage, not any offense, and the phrase \"Our clear revelations\" states the nature of what they rage at: the verses are clear, and the clarity is what enrages, because the clear truth leaves the denier no room, and the rage is the response of the cornered. The verse then gives the picture of the rage: \"as if they are going to snap at those who recite Our revelations to them\", and the word \"snap\" is the word of pouncing and seizing, and the verse says the rage is so strong that they are about to attack the reciters, and the picture is the verse's warning to the Prophet about the hostility that the recitation provokes. Then the verse gives the Prophet his answer: \"Say, \"Shall I inform you of something far more enraging than that? ˹It is˺ the Fire with which Allah has threatened those who disbelieve. What an evil destination!\"\" and the answer is the verse's irony: they rage at the recitation, and the Prophet is told to mention the thing that should enrage them far more, the Fire that awaits the disbelievers, and the word \"enraging\" turns their own weapon against them, and the verse says the rage at the truth is misplaced, because the thing worth fearing is not the verses but the Fire, and the verse closes with the exclamation: \"What an evil destination!\" and the exclamation is the verse's verdict on the end of the raging.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's explanation of the hostility the Prophet faced, and the explanation is the verse's psychology: the rage at the recitation is not a reasoned objection but the convulsion of the one who is losing the argument, and the verse says the convulsion will not save them, because the destination is the Fire, and the destination is the only thing their rage should have been directed at. The commentators note the verse's word \"enraging\" and its effect: the Prophet is not told to fear their rage or to answer it with rage, but to redirect it, and the redirection is the verse's teaching that the believer meets the rage of the deniers with the truth about the end, and the truth about the end is the only answer the rage deserves. The next verse will state the chapter's final argument against the idols, the parable of the fly, and the parable will close the case with the chapter's most humbling image.",
  "73": "\"O humanity! A lesson is set forth, so listen to it ˹carefully˺: those ˹idols˺ you invoke besides Allah can never create ˹so much as˺ a fly, even if they ˹all˺ were to come together for that\" opens the verse of the fly, and it is the chapter's last and most famous argument against the idols, and the verse calls all humanity to listen, and the call to listen marks the lesson's importance, and the word \"carefully\" asks for the full attention, because the lesson is the chapter's closing proof. The lesson itself is stated in its first half: the idols cannot create a fly, even if all of them gathered for the task, and the commentators note the choice of the fly: it is the smallest and most despised of creatures, and the verse says the idols cannot create even that, and the word \"even if they ˹all˺ were to come together\" closes the last escape, because the gathered power of every idol is still powerless before one fly, and the lesson is the chapter's reduction of the whole idol business to its true size, and the size is smaller than a fly. The second half of the verse completes the lesson: \"And if a fly were to snatch anything away from them, they cannot ˹even˺ retrieve it from the fly.\" The verse imagines the fly taking something from the idol, a speck of its food or its offering, and the verse says the idols cannot even take it back, and the picture is the final humiliation: the gods who cannot create a fly cannot recover from a fly, and the verse says their power is less than the fly's, and the two halves together are the complete measure of the idols: they cannot create the smallest thing, and they cannot recover the smallest loss. The verse then closes with the exclamation that summarizes the whole argument: \"How powerless are those who invoke and those invoked!\" and the word \"powerless\" is 'da'ufa,' the word of weakness, and the verse says the weakness is shared, the worshipper and the worshipped, and the exclamation is the chapter's verdict on both, and the verdict is the end of the argument. The same argument is stated elsewhere: \"But those ˹idols˺ they invoke besides Allah cannot create anything—they themselves are created.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's final word on the idols, and the word is the fly, and the choice of the smallest creature is the chapter's method at its sharpest: the argument does not rise to the heavens but sinks to the fly, and the sinking is the defeat, because the idols that failed before the fly have failed before everything, and the verse says the failure is shared by the worshippers, and the shared failure is the last lesson of the chapter's long case against false worship. The commentators note the verse's address to all humanity, and the address is the verse's claim: the lesson of the fly is not for one nation but for every human being, and the lesson is the chapter's parting proof, and the next verses will close the chapter with its final commands, and the commands will be the response to everything the lesson has shown.",
  "74": "\"They have not shown Allah the reverence He deserves. Surely Allah is All-Powerful, Almighty\" opens the chapter's closing section with a verdict on the idolaters, and the verdict is the measure they failed. The word \"reverence\" is 'qadr,' the word of measure and estimation, and the verse says they did not estimate God at His true measure, and the commentators explain the meaning: they worshipped Him beside the idols, and the partnership was the failure of the measurement, because the One who created everything cannot be placed beside the things He created, and the verse says the placement was the underestimation. The verse then states the two names that the underestimation ignored: \"Surely Allah is All-Powerful, Almighty\", and the word \"All-Powerful\" is 'qawiyy,' the strong, and the word \"Almighty\" is 'aziz,' the mighty, and the verse says the strength and the might are what they failed to measure, and the two names are the verse's correction of the measurement, and the correction is the chapter's last word on the idols, after the fly and the powerless and the falsehood, and the verse gathers the whole argument into the one failure: they did not measure God, and the verse now gives the measure. The same verdict is stated elsewhere: \"They have not shown Allah His proper reverence—when on the Day of Judgment the ˹whole˺ earth will be in His Grip, and the heavens will be rolled up in His Right Hand.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's transition from the arguments against the idols to the final commands, and the transition is a verdict: the argument is over, and the verdict is the underestimation, and the reader who has followed the chapter knows the measure now, and the measure is the two names at the verse's end, and the names are the foundation of everything the closing verses will command, because the commands are the response to the measure, and the response is the worship that the chapter has been describing since its opening. The next verse will state the choosing of the messengers, and the statement will show that the measure of God is also the measure of His messengers, and the two measures together are the chapter's final correction.",
  "75": "\"Allah selects messengers from both angels and people, for Allah is truly All-Hearing, All-Seeing\" states the choosing of the messengers, and the verse answers the old objection that a messenger should be an angel. The word \"selects\" is 'yastafi,' the word of choosing and purifying, and the verse says God selects His messengers from both kinds, the angels and the people, and the word \"both\" places the two selections side by side, and the commentators explain the answer folded into the verse: the selection is God's, and God selected human messengers for the human nations and angel messengers for the angelic tasks, and the one who objected that the messenger should be an angel has objected to the selection itself, and the verse says the selection was made by the One who sees the whole matter, and the two names that close the verse are the guarantee: \"Allah is truly All-Hearing, All-Seeing\", and the hearing and the seeing are the reasons the selection is right, because the One who hears every word and sees every state knows which messenger fits which nation, and the verse says the choosing was not a mistake but a design, and the design is the answer to the objection. The chapter has stated the same truth earlier, and the verse is the closing statement of it: the messengers are chosen, and the choosing is God's, and the objection to the choosing is the objection to the chooser.\n\nThe verse is also the chapter's preparation for its final verse, where the believers are told that the Messenger is a witness over them and they are witnesses over humanity, and the preparation matters: the messenger's rank is a selection, not an achievement, and the verse says the selection came from the hearing and the seeing of God, and the believer who understands the selection accepts the witness, and the acceptance is the chapter's last lesson on the messenger. The next verse will state the knowledge behind the selection, the knowledge of what is ahead and what is behind, and the statement will complete the chapter's description of the chooser, and the description will be the foundation of the final commands.",
  "76": "\"He knows what is ahead of them and what is behind them. And to Allah ˹all˺ matters will be returned ˹for judgment˺\" completes the description of the chooser, and the verse states the two directions of God's knowledge: what is ahead and what is behind, and the two directions cover all of time, the past of every creature and its future, and the verse says the selection of the previous verse was made inside this knowledge, and the knowing is the reason the selection cannot err, and the commentators explain the placement of the verse: the messengers were selected, and the selection stands on the knowledge, and the knowledge covers everything, and the verse says it plainly so that the believer trusts the arrangement. The second half of the verse states the destination of the arrangement: \"And to Allah ˹all˺ matters will be returned ˹for judgment˺\", and the word \"returned\" is 'turja'u,' the word of going back, and the verse says every matter returns to God, and the return is the judgement, and the verse places the knowledge and the return together, because the One who knows everything is the One to whom everything returns, and the return is the end of every affair the chapter has described, the disputes and the struggles and the selections, and the verse says the end is one, and the one is God.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's statement of the two facts that frame everything the closing verses will command: the knowledge that surrounds the present, and the return that closes the future, and the two facts together are the reason the commands that follow are worth obeying, because the obedience is known and the obedience is returned to, and the verse says both, and the reader is placed between the knowledge and the return, and the placement is the chapter's final framing of the believer's life. The next verse will state the commands themselves, the bowing and the prostration and the worship and the good, and the commands will be the response to the frame, and the response will be the chapter's last instruction before its great closing verse.",
  "77": "\"O believers! Bow down, prostrate yourselves, worship your Lord, and do ˹what is˺ good so that you may be successful\" opens the chapter's final address to the believers, and the verse gathers the acts of worship into one command. The address is \"O believers\", and the verse speaks to the community directly, and the commands follow in their order: \"Bow down, prostrate yourselves, worship your Lord, and do ˹what is˺ good\", and the order moves from the body to the heart to the world, and the commentators explain the sequence: the bowing and the prostration are the movements of the prayer, and the worship is the devotion of the heart that fills the movements, and the doing of good is the fruit that the devotion produces in the world, and the verse says the three together are the path to the one goal: \"so that you may be successful.\" The word \"successful\" is 'tuflihun,' the word of the farmer whose crop comes through, and the verse borrows the word from the harvest, and the success is the harvest of the Hereafter, and the verse says the bowing and the worship and the good are the planting, and the success is the crop, and the whole verse is the chapter's summary of the believer's task in four commands and one promise. The same success is promised elsewhere: \"O believers! Patiently endure, persevere, stand on guard, and be mindful of Allah, so you may be successful.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's preparation for its final verse, and the preparation is the gathering of the commands, and the gathering is the Quran's way of closing a chapter: the argument is over, and the verdict is stated, and the commands are given, and the verse places the commands in the believer's hands as the response to everything the chapter has shown, the Hour and the pilgrimage and the struggle and the fly, and the response is the bowing and the prostration and the worship and the good, and the verse says the response ends in success, and the success is the chapter's last promise before the great verse that follows. The next verse is the longest and most famous of the chapter's closing, and it will gather the whole history of the community into one command, and the command will be the chapter's final word.",
  "78": "\"Strive for ˹the cause of˺ Allah in the way He deserves, for ˹it is˺ He ˹Who˺ has chosen you, and laid upon you no hardship in the religion—the way of your forefather Abraham\" opens the final verse of the chapter, and it is one of the longest verses of the Quran, and its first word is the command that gives the verse its name. The word \"strive\" is 'jahidu,' the word of 'jihad,' the effort of the whole self, and the verse commands the believers to strive for God \"in the way He deserves,\" and the word \"deserves\" is the measure of the striving, and the commentators explain the meaning: the striving for God is a debt, and the verse commands the payment of the debt in its full measure, and the measure is the response to the measure of the previous verses, where the idolaters failed to estimate God, and the believers are commanded to estimate Him rightly in their effort. The verse then states the reasons for the command: \"for ˹it is˺ He ˹Who˺ has chosen you, and laid upon you no hardship in the religion\", and the choosing is the honour, and the absence of hardship is the mercy, and the verse says the religion itself contains no hardship, and the commentators explain that the word \"hardship\" means the impossible burden, and the religion was made possible, and the possibility is the reason the striving is owed. The verse then names the heritage: \"the way of your forefather Abraham\", and the word \"way\" is 'millah,' and the verse says the believers' religion is the way of Abraham, and the chapter that began with the pilgrimage of Abraham ends by claiming his way, and the claim is the chapter's completion of its theme.\n\nThe verse then continues with the naming: \"˹It is Allah˺ Who named you ‘the ones who submit’ ˹in the˺ earlier ˹Scriptures˺ and in this ˹Quran˺,\" and the word \"the ones who submit\" is 'al-muslimin,' and the verse says the name of the believers was given by God in the earlier Scriptures and in the Quran, and the naming is the honour the verse places before the duty: \"so that the Messenger may be a witness over you, and that you may be witnesses over humanity\", and the witness is the task the name carries, and the verse says the community exists to testify, and the testimony is the striving the verse opened with. The verse then closes with the four commands and the praise: \"So establish prayer, pay alms-tax, and hold fast to Allah. He ˹alone˺ is your Guardian. What an excellent Guardian, and what an excellent Helper!\" and the commands are the chapter's final summary, and the praise is the chapter's final word, and the word is the Guardian, and the verse ends the chapter on the name of the One who guards, and the end is the chapter's answer to everything it described: the believer who strives and prays and gives and holds fast has the best Guardian, and the Guardian is the last word of the chapter of the pilgrimage."
};
